exports[`snapshots of the layout IR as text equipment/instrument-inventory.md @ 80 1`] = `
"▌ Instrument inventory — what we own, where it is, who looks after it

A running overview of the instruments, sensors and computers at Halden Ridge
Observatory. It is the first place to look before a night, a repair, or a grant
report that asks "what do you actually have?"

The detailed manuals live next to each instrument's folder. This note only says
what, where, who and how healthy — and it is updated whenever someone swaps a
part, not once a year.

▌ Vocabulary

┌────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Term       │ Meaning                                                         │
╞════════════╪═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╡
│ instrument │ Anything that produces science data: a camera, the              │
│            │ spectrograph, the filter wheel.                                 │
├────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ sensor     │ Something that watches the site rather than the sky: Sable,     │
│            │ Quill, Tern.                                                    │
├────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ host       │ One of the three ridge computers. Named ridge-*, never renamed, │
│            │ never repurposed.                                               │
├────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ owner      │ The person who answers for it. Not the only person allowed to   │
│            │ touch it.                                                       │
├────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ custodian  │ Whoever holds the spare parts and the manuals for it — usually  │
│            │ Tomasz.                                                         │
├────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ night log  │ The per-night record kept by the night assistant in             │
│            │ logs/nights/.                                                   │
├────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ red tag    │ A physical tag hung on anything that must not be powered on     │
│            │ until it is removed.                                            │
└────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

▌ Where things live


 Halden Ridge Observatory
 ├── Main Dome (5.2 m)
 │   └── Aster 0.61 m RC ── FW-8 ── HCAM-2        (Lyra swaps in for HCAM-2)
 ├── East Roll-off
 │   └── Brill 0.35 m SCT ── spare CCD on loan    (outreach nights only)
 ├── Visitor Pavilion
 │   ├── Corvid 0.2 m refractor                   (public eyepiece sessions)
 │   └── basement: Server Room
 │       ├── ridge-ctl    control PC, runs nightjar and the night scripts
 │       ├── ridge-arch   archive server, stacks (PostgreSQL) + raw FITS
 │       └── ridge-web    public site, weather page, meteor gallery
 ├── Workshop
 │   └── Dunmore 0.4 m Newtonian                  (in pieces, see refurb log)
 └── Mast on the north knoll: Sable, Quill, Tern


The mast is 40 m from the dome on purpose: close enough for one cable run, far
enough that dome heat does not spoil the seeing figures.

▌ Ownership

• Tomasz Wierzbicki owns every piece of hardware on this page unless a row says
  otherwise. He also keeps the red tags, which live in a biscuit tin in the
  Workshop.
• Priya Raman owns the three hosts and everything that runs on them: halley,
  nightjar, stacks.
• Lena Hofstetter owns Corvid and the public weather page on ridge-web.
• Rosa Delgado is the first responder at night for anything with a status marker
  below ✅.
• Min-jun Seo keeps this note current, as part of the intern handbook.

  Ownership is about answering questions, not gatekeeping. Anyone trained may
  open a panel; they write a line in the night log when they do.

▌ Science instruments

┌────────────┬─────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────┐
│ Instrument │ What it is                  │ Status and notes                  │
╞════════════╪═════════════════════════════╪═══════════════════════════════════╡
│ HCAM-2     │ Thermoelectrically cooled   │ ✅ In service. Dark current back  │
│            │ 4k×4k CCD on Aster, 15 µm   │ within spec after the March       │
│            │ pixels, runs at −30 °C on a │ desiccant change.                 │
│            │ normal night                │                                   │
├────────────┼─────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────┤
│ Lyra       │ Low-resolution long-slit    │ ⚠️ Usable, but the slit mechanism │
│            │ spectrograph, R ≈ 900,      │ sticks below −5 °C. Warm it with  │
│            │ swaps onto Aster in place   │ the dome heater first.            │
│            │ of HCAM-2                   │                                   │
├────────────┼─────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────┤
│ FW-8       │ Eight-position filter       │ 🔧 Position 6 (H-alpha)           │
│            │ wheel: U B V R I, H-alpha,  │ misreports its index roughly once │
│            │ OIII and clear, between     │ in forty moves. Home it twice.    │
│            │ Aster and the camera        │                                   │
├────────────┼─────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────┤
│ Spare CCD  │ Older 2k×2k camera, on loan │ ✅ Fine for public nights. Not    │
│            │ to the roll-off for         │ calibrated for survey work, and   │
│            │ outreach use on Brill       │ nobody should pretend otherwise.  │
└────────────┴─────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────┘

▌ Site sensors

┌────────┬───────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Sensor │ What it is                    │ Status and notes                    │
╞════════╪═══════════════════════════════╪═════════════════════════════════════╡
│ Sable  │ Weather station on the mast:  │ ✅ Feeds nightjar every 30 s. The   │
│        │ wind, humidity, rain, sky     │ rain sensor is the only thing       │
│        │ temperature, pressure         │ allowed to close the dome alone.    │
├────────┼───────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Quill  │ Differential image-motion     │ ⚠️ Reads about 0.2″ high since the  │
│        │ seeing monitor with its own   │ tube was knocked in June.           │
│        │ small tube                    │ Re-collimation is on the list.      │
├────────┼───────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Tern   │ Fisheye all-sky camera for    │ ✅ Captures every 20 s from dusk to │
│        │ cloud cover and the Meteor    │ dawn; detections go to the meteor   │
│        │ Watch project                 │ gallery on ridge-web.               │
└────────┴───────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────┘

▌ Hosts

┌────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────┐
│ Host       │ Role                                         │ State            │
╞════════════╪══════════════════════════════════════════════╪══════════════════╡
│ ridge-ctl  │ Control PC: nightjar scheduler, night        │ ✅ Healthy       │
│            │ start/stop scripts, device drivers           │                  │
├────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────┤
│ ridge-arch │ Archive: stacks database, raw and reduced    │ ⚠️ Disk 78 %     │
│            │ FITS, nightly backups                        │ full             │
├────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────┤
│ ridge-web  │ Public site, weather page, meteor gallery at │ ✅ Healthy       │
│            │ https://ridge.example.org                    │                  │
└────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────┘

All three are on the pavilion UPS, which buys about 25 minutes — long enough for
nightjar to park Aster and close the dome, not long enough to finish a reduction
run.

▌ Status (2026-09-28)

▌ What each marker means

• ✅ in service, nothing to do
• ⚠️ usable with a known workaround, written in the row
• 🔧 in repair or waiting for a part; use only if the row says how
• ❌ out of service and red-tagged; do not power on

▌ Open problems — detail

┌────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────┐
│ Item       │ Problem                             │ Plan                      │
╞════════════╪═════════════════════════════════════╪═══════════════════════════╡
│ FW-8 index │ The optical index sensor on         │ Tomasz suspects a loose   │
│ drift ⚠️   │ position 6 sometimes reports        │ index flag; a replacement │
│            │ position 5 after a move, so a frame │ is ordered from the       │
│            │ labelled H-alpha is in fact taken   │ maker. Until it arrives,  │
│            │ through OIII. halley catches it     │ nightjar homes the wheel  │
│            │ only when the sky background looks  │ twice before every        │
│            │ wrong, which is not every time.     │ H-alpha sequence and      │
│            │ Rosa has seen it four times since   │ halley flags any frame    │
│            │ August, always after a long slew on │ whose header and          │
│            │ a cold night.                       │ background disagree.      │
├────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┤
│ Lyra slit  │ Below −5 °C the slit carriage stops │ Run the dome heater for   │
│ sticking   │ a few hundred microns short, so the │ 20 minutes before         │
│ 🔧         │ first spectrum of a cold night has  │ mounting Lyra. Longer     │
│            │ a slit wider than the header claims │ term, swap the grease for │
│            │ and the line widths look fatter     │ a low-temperature one     │
│            │ than they are.                      │ when the unit comes off   │
│            │                                     │ in November.              │
├────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┤
│ Quill      │ After the June knock the seeing     │ Min-jun will re-collimate │
│ offset ⚠️  │ values run about 0.2″ high compared │ it on the next clear      │
│            │ with stars measured on HCAM-2       │ afternoon with Tomasz     │
│            │ frames the same night. Nothing      │ watching. Until then      │
│            │ breaks, but the seeing plot on the  │ halley applies a flat     │
│            │ public page looks gloomier than the │ −0.2″ correction and says │
│            │ sky.                                │ so in the plot caption.   │
├────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┤
│ ridge-arch │ The raw FITS partition is at 78 %.  │ Priya will move 2024 raw  │
│ disk ⚠️    │ At the current rate of the Variable │ frames to the             │
│            │ Star Survey it reaches 90 % around  │ cold-storage drive, then  │
│            │ mid-November, which is where the    │ decide between a second   │
│            │ backup job starts refusing to run   │ disk and compressing      │
│            │ and leaves a very polite and very   │ older raw data. Either    │
│            │ easy to miss message in its log.    │ way the decision is       │
│            │                                     │ written up before the     │
│            │                                     │ partition reaches 85 %.   │
├────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┤
│ Dunmore ❌ │ The 0.4 m Newtonian is in pieces in │ The recoated primary is   │
│            │ the Workshop for the Dunmore        │ due back in October. The  │
│            │ Refurbishment: primary out for      │ refurb log in             │
│            │ recoating, secondary cell rebuilt,  │ projects/dunmore/ has the │
│            │ tube rings still to be machined. It │ full list; this row only  │
│            │ is red-tagged so nobody wires it to │ exists so the inventory   │
│            │ a mount by mistake.                 │ is honest.                │
└────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────┘

▌ Smaller niceties (not blocking anything)

• A label printer for the Server Room. The three hosts look identical, and twice
  this year someone has unplugged the wrong one. Lena has offered her outreach
  label maker, which prints in a cheerful font nobody asked for.
• A shared spares list. Spare fuses, filter-wheel belts and the one spare Sable
  anemometer cup are in three different drawers. A single table in this note
  would do.

▌ Smallest path to all green

1. ✅ HCAM-2 desiccant changed (March)
2. ✅ Sable rain sensor recalibrated (July)
3. ⏳ FW-8 index flag replaced — part ordered, due mid-October
4. 🔲 Quill re-collimated on a clear afternoon
5. 🔲 ridge-arch raw partition below 70 % — move the 2024 raw frames, then
   decide on the second disk
6. 🔲 Lyra re-greased when it comes off Aster in November
7. 🔲 Dunmore reassembled and moved out of this note into a normal row

▌ Spares

┌──────────────────────┬────────────────────┬───────┐
│ Part                 │ Drawer             │ Count │
╞══════════════════════╪════════════════════╪═══════╡
│ FW-8 drive belt      │ Workshop, drawer 3 │ 2     │
│ Sable anemometer cup │ Workshop, drawer 5 │ 1     │
│ UPS fuses (10 A)     │ Server Room shelf  │ 6     │
└──────────────────────┴────────────────────┴───────┘

• Take one, write one line in the night log.
• Take the last one, tell Tomasz the same day.

▌ Photos

Each instrument is meant to have a photo beside its row, so a new volunteer can
find it in the dark. None of these have been taken yet; the links below are
placeholders, and they will show as missing until Min-jun's photo walk-round in
October.

▣ Lyra slit assembly <images/lyra-slit.png>

The Tern housing, from below, with the heater cable visible (no caption yet):

▣ tern-frame.jpg <images/tern-frame.jpg>

The flat field that should be pinned here, for comparing against a suspect
night:

▣ the missing flat field <missing-flat-field.png>

▌ History

This inventory started as a sheet of paper taped inside the Workshop door,
listing which cables went where. Ada asked for it to become a note after a
visiting astronomer spent an evening looking for a filter wheel that had been in
the roll-off for a month. Last full walk-round by Min-jun Seo and Tomasz
Wierzbicki on 2026-09-28.

Changes to this note follow one rule: the row changes the same day the hardware
does. A part swapped on Tuesday and written up on Friday is how the filter wheel
went missing in the first place. If the change is large — a new instrument, a
host retired, a telescope moved between buildings — add a dated line here as
well, so the next walk-round knows what to look for.

Questions about a row go to its owner first, then to Tomasz. Questions about the
note itself go to Min-jun."
`;

exports[`snapshots of the layout IR as text equipment/telescope-comparison.md @ 80 1`] = `
"▌ Telescope comparison

Working note for the equipment review, March 2026. Tomasz owns the numbers; Ada
wants a recommendation before the trust meets.

Scope: the four scopes we run today and the two candidates quoted by the vendors
at example.com.

▌ Summary

The short version is that Aster is still the backbone of the Variable Star
Survey and nothing we can afford beats it, Brill is fine but its mount is tired
and the roll-off shed floods its electronics with dew every autumn, Corvid does
exactly what outreach needs and should be left alone, and Dunmore is either a
bargain once the refurbishment is finished or a very heavy lesson in sunk costs,
depending on who you ask and how late in the evening you ask them, so this note
tries to put the actual numbers side by side before anyone signs a purchase
order.

Nothing below is final. Corrections go to Tomasz.

▌ Fleet at a glance

┌────┬─────────┬──────────────────┬──────────┬────────────┬───────────┬────────┐
│ ID │ Name    │ Optics           │ Aperture │ Focal      │ Home      │ Status │
│    │         │                  │          │ ratio      │           │        │
╞════╪═════════╪══════════════════╪══════════╪════════════╪═══════════╪════════╡
│ T1 │ Aster   │ Ritchey–Chrétien │ 0.61 m   │ f/8        │ Main Dome │ up     │
│ T2 │ Brill   │ SCT              │ 0.35 m   │ f/10       │ Roll-off  │ up     │
│ T3 │ Corvid  │ Refractor        │ 0.2 m    │ f/7        │ Pavilion  │ up     │
│ T4 │ Dunmore │ Newtonian        │ 0.4 m    │ f/4.5      │ Workshop  │ down   │
└────┴─────────┴──────────────────┴──────────┴────────────┴───────────┴────────┘

▌ Pros and cons

Rosa and Priya each filled in a column. Their sentences are kept whole, on
purpose.

┌───────────┬──────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────┐
│ Telescope │ What it is good at               │ Where it lets us down         │
╞═══════════╪══════════════════════════════════╪═══════════════════════════════╡
│ Aster     │ Deep, stable photometry for the  │ Slow to slew and expensive to │
│           │ survey: with HCAM-2 and the FW-8 │ fix; when the secondary       │
│           │ filter wheel it holds focus      │ mirror cell drifts, nobody    │
│           │ across a whole night, reaches    │ but Tomasz can collimate it,  │
│           │ fainter targets than anything    │ and we lose three or four     │
│           │ else on the ridge, and its       │ clear nights while the        │
│           │ pointing model survives a full   │ replacement bolts are         │
│           │ season without a rebuild unless  │ machined in the Workshop.     │
│           │ someone bumps the dome.          │                               │
├───────────┼──────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┤
│ Brill     │ Quick to open, quick to point,   │ The mount has backlash in     │
│           │ and the obvious backup whenever  │ declination that the guider   │
│           │ Aster is down; the roll-off roof │ only partly hides, the        │
│           │ means it is cooled to ambient    │ corrector plate dews over by  │
│           │ within twenty minutes, which the │ midnight on humid nights, and │
│           │ dome never manages in spring.    │ the shed floods its cable     │
│           │                                  │ tray whenever the gutter on   │
│           │                                  │ the north side is blocked.    │
├───────────┼──────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┤
│ Dunmore   │ Wide field and fast optics; once │ Currently in pieces. The      │
│           │ the new primary cell is fitted   │ refurbishment has already run │
│           │ it should be the best meteor and │ eleven weeks over plan, the   │
│           │ comet instrument we own, and it  │ tube is heavy enough to need  │
│           │ could take the Lyra spectrograph │ two people and a hoist, and   │
│           │ without the flexure problems     │ we have no pier for it yet.   │
│           │ Brill has.                       │                               │
└───────────┴──────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────┘

▌ Candidate replacements

┌────────┬───────────┬─────────────┬────────────┬────────┬───────────┬─────────┐
│ Option │ Aperture  │ Mount       │ Camera     │ Price  │ Lead time │ Verdict │
╞════════╪═══════════╪═════════════╪════════════╪════════╪═══════════╪═════════╡
│ C1     │ 0.5 m CDK │ Direct      │ Reuse      │ 48,000 │ 14 weeks  │ maybe   │
│        │           │ drive       │ HCAM-2     │        │           │         │
├────────┼───────────┼─────────────┼────────────┼────────┼───────────┼─────────┤
│ C2     │ 0.4 m RC  │ Harmonic    │ New CMOS   │ 31,500 │ 9 weeks   │ no      │
└────────┴───────────┴─────────────┴────────────┴────────┴───────────┴─────────┘

▌ Maintenance matrix

┌─────────────┬────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────┬──────────┐
│ Task        │ Owner  │ Notes                                      │ Every    │
╞═════════════╪════════╪════════════════════════════════════════════╪══════════╡
│ Collimation │ Tomasz │ Aster only; needs the laser jig from the   │ 3 months │
│             │        │ Workshop and one calm evening with a star  │          │
│             │        │ near the zenith.                           │          │
├─────────────┼────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────┤
│ Dew heaters │ Rosa   │ Check current draw on Brill and Corvid     │ 6 months │
│             │        │ before the autumn campaign, replace any    │          │
│             │        │ strip reading below 0.8 A.                 │          │
├─────────────┼────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────┤
│ Mirror wash │ Tomasz │ Distilled water only, no detergent, and    │ 1 year   │
│             │        │ never on the same day as a public night.   │          │
└─────────────┴────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────┴──────────┘

▌ Mount notes

• Mounts, as reported by the night assistants:

  ┌─────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ Mount               │ Observation                                          │
  ╞═════════════════════╪══════════════════════════════════════════════════════╡
  │ Brill's German      │ Declination backlash of roughly 40 arcseconds, worse │
  │ equatorial mount    │ after the shed has been closed for a week of rain.   │
  └─────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

• Aster's fork mount has no complaints.

▌ Night check script

                                                                           bash
 for t in aster brill corvid; do ssh ridge-ctl "halley status --scope $t --sinc
↪e 18:00 | tee -a /var/log/hro/$t.log"; done


▌ Open questions

• Whether the trust would fund C1 outright or only through the Dark Sky Campaign
  grant at example.org, which pays in two instalments.
• Pier for Dunmore.
  ◦ If it goes in the East Roll-off, Brill has to move, and nobody has measured
    whether the roof clears a 0.4 m tube.
  ◦ Min-jun is drafting three pier layouts.

▌ Next steps

1. Tomasz confirms the C1 quote by 15 April
2. Priya estimates what a second camera costs the halley pipeline in storage and
   processing time
3. Rosa logs Brill's dew incidents for one more month
4. Lena checks whether Corvid can stay in the Pavilion year-round
5. Ada takes the recommendation to the trust
6. Kenji reviews the photometry comparison"
`;

exports[`snapshots of the layout IR as text handbook/operations-handbook.md @ 80 1`] = `
"▌ Halden Ridge Observatory — Operations Handbook

Numbered log of operational decisions and standing procedures. Add a new entry
when a choice is made or revisited; never renumber. Superseded entries stay,
marked, so the reasoning survives.

▌ O1: One handbook, one numbered log (2025-02)

Decision: Every operational decision lives in this file as a numbered entry, O1
onward. Why: Before this, decisions were scattered across the workshop
whiteboard, three spreadsheets and Tomasz's memory. A single log is searchable
and diffable, and new volunteers can read it front to back on a cloudy night
(there are plenty).

Consequences: Entries are never renumbered. A reversed decision gets a new entry
and the old one gets a "superseded by" note at the top.

▌ O2: Night assistant owns the night (2025-02)

Decision: The night assistant on shift has final say over whether the dome
opens, closes or stays shut, regardless of who else is on site. Why: Visiting
astronomers are, understandably, keen. The night assistant is the one who has
watched the Sable readings all afternoon and knows the dome's quirks.

Consequences: Rosa Delgado, as senior night assistant, trains every new
assistant on the same checklist (O4). Directors and engineers defer on the
night; they argue about it at the Monday meeting instead.

▌ O3: Observatory time is UTC, everywhere (2025-02)

Decision: All logs, file names, schedules and FITS headers use UTC. Local time
appears only on the public events calendar. Why: Marrowby observes daylight
saving; the sky does not. Two sessions in 2024 were logged an hour off and a
variable star light curve grew a mysterious step.

▌ O4: The opening checklist (2025-03)

Decision: Opening the Main Dome follows a fixed checklist, read aloud when two
people are present and ticked in the night log when alone. Why: The dome is 5.2
m of steel with a slit that does not care about your fingers.

The checklist, in order:

• Confirm Sable reports humidity below 85 % and wind below 12 m/s (gusts below
  18 m/s).
• Check the Tern all-sky frame for cloud on the horizon upwind.
• Confirm no one is on the catwalk or inside the dome ring.
• Power on the dome controller and wait for the green READY lamp.
• Unlock the slit, open to 25 %, pause ten seconds, listen.
• Open fully. If the shutter motor stutters, close and log it.
• Uncover Aster's primary mirror only after the dome has equalised for 20
  minutes.
• Start the Quill seeing monitor and note the first FWHM value in the log.
• Start nightjar with the night's plan (O14).
• Write the opening time and initials in the night log.

▎ Rule of thumb from Rosa: if you had to read Sable twice to convince yourself,
▎ the answer is no.

▎ ⚠ Dome safety
▎ The slit and the dome ring move with enough force to break a wrist, and
▎ neither has a sensor that notices a hand. Nobody touches the slit edge or the
▎ ring rail while the controller shows READY, and nobody stands on the catwalk
▎ while the dome rotates (O8).

▎ ✦ Night assistant shortcut
▎ night-start.sh (O11) runs the first four checks for you and prints them in one
▎ block. Read the block aloud instead of the first four lines of the list; the
▎ rest are still done by hand.

▌ O5: The closing checklist (2025-03)

Decision: Closing mirrors opening, in reverse, with two additions: park the
telescope before the slit moves, and confirm the dome heater is off. Why: In
November 2024 the heater ran for four days and the Main Dome smelled like toast.

• Stop nightjar gracefully (nightjar stop --drain), wait for the current
  exposure to finish.
• Park Aster at the zenith-park position.
• Cover the primary mirror.
• Close the slit, then rotate the dome to the home position.
• Confirm the dome heater lamp is off.
• Stop Quill.
• Run the end-of-night script (O12).
• Write the closing time and initials in the night log.

▌ O6: Weather limits are numbers, not moods (2025-03)

Decision: Hard weather limits are written down and enforced by the dome
controller where it can; the night assistant enforces the rest. Why: "It looked
fine" is not a data point.

┌──────────────────┬────────────────┬───────────────────┬──────────────────┐
│ Parameter        │ Open allowed   │ Close immediately │ Source           │
╞══════════════════╪════════════════╪═══════════════════╪══════════════════╡
│ Humidity         │ < 85 %         │ ≥ 90 %            │ Sable            │
│ Wind (sustained) │ < 12 m/s       │ ≥ 15 m/s          │ Sable            │
│ Wind (gust)      │ < 18 m/s       │ ≥ 20 m/s          │ Sable            │
│ Rain sensor      │ dry            │ any wet reading   │ Sable            │
│ Cloud (all-sky)  │ < 40 % covered │ > 70 % covered    │ Tern             │
│ Dew point margin │ > 3 °C         │ < 1.5 °C          │ Sable (computed) │
│ Seeing (FWHM)    │ any            │ n/a               │ Quill            │
└──────────────────┴────────────────┴───────────────────┴──────────────────┘

Consequences: The controller closes the slit on its own when rain or humidity
cross the close line. It does not know about cloud; the night assistant does.

▌ O7: Sable is the weather source of truth (2025-03)

Decision: When the Sable station disagrees with a phone weather app, Sable wins.
Why: Sable sits on the mast 12 m from the dome. The phone forecast is for
Marrowby, 400 m lower and in a valley that makes its own fog.

Consequences: If Sable is offline, the dome stays shut. There is no "use the
phone" fallback.

▌ O8: Two-person rule for the catwalk (2025-04)

Decision: Nobody goes onto the outer catwalk alone, day or night. Why: It is 7 m
up, it is often icy, and the handrail was installed in 1987.

▌ O9: Control PC is ridge-ctl, and only it moves hardware (2025-04)

Decision: Only ridge-ctl sends commands to the mount, the dome and the filter
wheel. Laptops may view, but never drive. Why: Two clients once commanded the
mount at the same time. The mount chose neither.

Consequences: Remote observers connect to ridge-ctl through the gateway (O22),
not directly to hardware. The hardware serial ports are bound to ridge-ctl in
the udev rules.

▌ O10: Host roles (2025-04)

Decision: Three hosts, each with one job.

┌────────────┬────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────┬──────────────┐
│ Host       │ Role                       │ Location            │ Backed up by │
╞════════════╪════════════════════════════╪═════════════════════╪══════════════╡
│ ridge-ctl  │ Drives hardware, runs      │ Main Dome control   │ ridge-arch   │
│            │ nightjar                   │ room                │              │
├────────────┼────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────┼──────────────┤
│ ridge-arch │ Archive (stacks), halley   │ Server Room         │ offsite disk │
│            │ reductions                 │                     │              │
├────────────┼────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────┼──────────────┤
│ ridge-web  │ Public site, events        │ Server Room         │ ridge-arch   │
│            │ calendar, webcam           │                     │              │
└────────────┴────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────┴──────────────┘

Why: When the public site and the archive shared a machine, a busy open-night
webcam page slowed the reductions to a crawl.

▌ O11: Start-of-night script (2025-04)

Decision: A single script prepares ridge-ctl for the night. It is idempotent:
running it twice is harmless. Why: The old procedure was eleven manual steps,
and step seven was frequently forgotten.

                                                                             sh
 #!/bin/sh
 # night-start.sh — prepare ridge-ctl for observing. Safe to re-run.
 set -eu
 NIGHT=$(date -u +%Y%m%d)
 DATA=/data/raw/$NIGHT
 mkdir -p "$DATA"
 halley-cooler --target -25 --wait 600 || { echo "HCAM-2 did not reach -25 C in
↪ 10 min; check the chiller and the log before observing" >&2; exit 1; }
 fw8 home && fw8 select clear
 quill start --log /var/log/quill/$NIGHT.log
 nightjar check-plan /etc/nightjar/plans/$NIGHT.toml
 echo "night $NIGHT ready at $(date -u +%H:%M) UTC"


Consequences: If the cooler step fails, the script stops. That is on purpose.

▌ O12: End-of-night script (2025-04)

Decision: The end-of-night script copies raw frames to ridge-arch, verifies
checksums, and only then marks the night as transferred. Why: A raw frame that
exists in one place does not exist.

                                                                             sh
 #!/bin/sh
 # night-end.sh — ship the night's raw data to the archive and verify it.
 set -eu
 NIGHT=\${1:-$(date -u -d '12 hours ago' +%Y%m%d)}
 SRC=/data/raw/$NIGHT
 rsync -a --checksum "$SRC/" "ridge-arch:/archive/incoming/$NIGHT/"
 ssh ridge-arch "cd /archive/incoming/$NIGHT && sha256sum -c MANIFEST.sha256" |
↪| exit 2
 ssh ridge-arch "stacks-ingest --night $NIGHT --source aster --instrument hcam2
↪ --notify ops@example.org --on-failure=keep-raw"
 touch "$SRC/.transferred"


Raw frames stay on ridge-ctl for 14 days after .transferred appears, then a cron
job removes them. Fourteen, not seven, because of the Easter 2025 incident
(O31).

▌ O13: Night log format (2025-05)

Decision: The night log is a plain text file per night, one line per event, UTC
timestamp first. Why: It gets read at 04:00 by a tired person and grepped at
14:00 by a curious one. Plain text serves both.

Each line looks like:

                                                                           text
 2025-05-14T21:42Z RD  open   dome open, Sable 71% 4.2m/s, Quill FWHM 1.9"
 2025-05-14T21:44Z RD  note   Kenji on remote via gateway, observing VSS field
↪112
 2025-05-14T23:10Z RD  warn   humidity rising 82%, watching
 2025-05-15T00:03Z RD  close  humidity 90%, controller closed slit, confirmed m
↪anually


The second column is initials, the third is one of open, close, note, warn,
fault.

▌ O14: nightjar plans are files, reviewed like code (2025-05)

Decision: Each night's observing plan is a TOML file under /etc/nightjar/plans/,
kept in the ridge-ops repository and reviewed by a second person before 17:00
UTC on the day. Why: A typo in a plan once pointed Aster at the Sun's position
for "twilight flats". The safety interlock caught it. We would rather catch it
at review.

                                                                           toml
 # /etc/nightjar/plans/20250514.toml
 [night]
 date      = "2025-05-14"
 observer  = "RD"
 telescope = "aster"
 camera    = "hcam2"

 [[block]]
 name     = "vss-field-112"
 program  = "VSS"
 filters  = ["V", "R"]
 exposure = 90
 repeats  = 12
 priority = 2

 [[block]]
 name     = "twilight-flats-dawn"
 kind     = "flat"
 filters  = ["B", "V", "R", "I"]
 sun_alt  = [-10.0, -4.0]   # degrees; the interlock refuses anything above -3.
↪0 regardless of what this file says


Consequences:

• nightjar refuses a plan with no observer.
• A plan not merged by 17:00 falls back to the standing VSS plan.
• Visiting astronomers submit plans through Priya, who reviews them with the
  visitor, not for them.

▌ O15: The standing VSS plan (2025-05)

Decision: When nothing else is scheduled, Aster runs the Variable Star Survey
standing plan. Why: Four hundred variable stars do not observe themselves, and
an idle telescope on a clear night is the most expensive thing on the ridge.

The standing plan rotates through fields by cadence class:

┌───────────────┬───────┬───────────────┬────────────┬──────────────────┐
│ Cadence class │ Stars │ Revisit       │ Filters    │ Typical exposure │
╞═══════════════╪═══════╪═══════════════╪════════════╪══════════════════╡
│ Fast          │ 38    │ every night   │ V          │ 30 s             │
│ Medium        │ 141   │ every 3 days  │ V, R       │ 60 s             │
│ Slow          │ 196   │ every 10 days │ B, V, R    │ 90 s             │
│ Calibration   │ 27    │ every night   │ B, V, R, I │ 20 s             │
└───────────────┴───────┴───────────────┴────────────┴──────────────────┘

▌ O16: Filter wheel slots are fixed (2025-05)

Decision: The FW-8 slot assignment does not change without an entry here. Why:
halley maps slot numbers to filter names from a config file. Swapping filters
physically without updating the config gives you beautiful H-alpha frames
labelled "U".

┌──────┬─────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Slot │ Filter  │ Notes                                             │
╞══════╪═════════╪═══════════════════════════════════════════════════╡
│ 1    │ U       │ Low throughput; long exposures only               │
│ 2    │ B       │                                                   │
│ 3    │ V       │ Primary survey filter                             │
│ 4    │ R       │                                                   │
│ 5    │ I       │ Fringing on HCAM-2 above 800 nm; halley defringes │
│ 6    │ H-alpha │ 7 nm bandpass                                     │
│ 7    │ OIII    │ 8.5 nm bandpass                                   │
│ 8    │ clear   │ Focus and pointing only, never science            │
└──────┴─────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

▌ Swapping a filter

1. Log the intent in the night log with note.
2. Park Aster, home FW-8, power it down.
3. Swap the filter, wearing gloves, holding it by the cell.
4. Update /etc/halley/filters.toml and commit it to ridge-ops.
5. Power FW-8 up and take one clear-sky test frame in the new slot.
6. Add a line to this entry with the date and your initials.

▌ O17: Calibration frames every night (2025-06)

Decision: Every observing night gets bias, dark and flat frames, even if the
science was washed out. Why: Calibrations are cheap, stable-ish, and the only
way to notice a failing cooler before it ruins a month of photometry.

• 21 bias frames at start of night.
• 11 darks at each science exposure length used, taken at end of night.
• Twilight flats in each filter used, dusk or dawn, whichever is clear.
• If both twilights are cloudy, dome flats against the flat-field screen; mark
  them dome=1.

▎ Dome flats are a last resort. They are consistently 2 % off twilight flats in
▎ B, and nobody has worked out why. Priya's bet is the screen lamp; Tomasz's bet
▎ is the screen.

▌ O18: halley versions are pinned per season (2025-06)

Decision: The pipeline version used for reductions is pinned at the start of
each observing season (September) and only patch releases go in mid-season. Why:
A mid-season change to the aperture photometry code shifted every magnitude by
0.004. Small, real, and very annoying to explain in a paper.

Consequences: Feature work on halley happens on a branch and lands between
seasons. Priya keeps a changelog on the archive host; the latest release notes
are also mirrored at https://example.org/halley/releases for visiting
astronomers.

▌ O19: Archive layout on ridge-arch (2025-06)

Decision: The archive is organised by night, then telescope, then instrument.
Why: Most questions are "what happened on the night of…". Organising by target
made that question a query; organising by night makes it ls.

                                                                           text
 /archive/
   incoming/<YYYYMMDD>/            # raw, as shipped by night-end.sh
   raw/<YYYYMMDD>/<telescope>/<instrument>/
   reduced/<YYYYMMDD>/<telescope>/<instrument>/
   calib/<YYYYMMDD>/master_{bias,dark_<exp>,flat_<filter>}.fits
   catalogue/                      # stacks database dumps, nightly
   public/                         # anything the outreach site may show


▌ O20: stacks database backups (2025-07)

Decision: The stacks PostgreSQL database is dumped nightly at 12:00 UTC and
copied to the offsite disk every Sunday. Why: Noon is the quietest hour on the
ridge: nobody observing, nobody reducing, the visitor pavilion still shut.

• Nightly: pg_dump to /archive/catalogue/, kept 30 days.
• Weekly: the Sunday dump goes to the offsite disk in the director's office in
  Marrowby.
• Quarterly: Priya restores one dump to a scratch database and runs the
  consistency checks.

▎ A backup you have never restored is a rumour.

▌ O21: Dunmore stays off the network during refurbishment (2025-07)

Decision: The 0.4 m Dunmore Newtonian is not connected to ridge-ctl or the
observatory network until the refurbishment is signed off. Why: Half-rebuilt
mounts do surprising things when a scheduler finds them.

Consequences: Dunmore test runs use a laptop on the workshop bench network,
which is physically separate.

▌ O22: Remote access through one gateway (2025-07)

Decision: All remote access goes through a single gateway host with per-person
keys. There is no VPN to the hardware network. Why: Visiting astronomers log in
from hotel Wi-Fi on four continents. One door is easier to watch than several.

▌ The gateway client block

Remote observers add this to their SSH client configuration. The gateway name is
a placeholder here; the real one is in the welcome pack.

                                                                           text
 # Halden Ridge — remote observing. Replace the key path with your own.
 Host hro-gw
     HostName gw.example.invalid
     User observer
     IdentityFile ~/.ssh/hro_ed25519
     IdentitiesOnly yes
     ServerAliveInterval 30

 Host ridge-ctl
     ProxyJump hro-gw
     User observer
     LocalForward 5901 localhost:5901   # the nightjar status display; view-onl
↪y unless the night assistant grants control


▌ Who gets a key

• Staff: permanent key, reviewed each January.
• Visiting astronomers: a key valid for their allocation plus three days.
• Students: a key valid for their placement, view-only on the scheduler.
• Volunteers: no remote key; they observe on site with a night assistant.

▌ Revoking a key

1. Remove the public key from the gateway's authorized_keys.
2. Log the revocation in ridge-ops/access.md with the date and reason.
3. Tell the person, politely, even if they already know.

▌ O23: Public webcam delays by ten minutes (2025-08)

Decision: The public webcam on ridge-web shows images from the Tern all-sky
camera with a ten-minute delay, and never shows the inside of the dome. Why: The
delay stops the webcam being used to time visits to the car park at night. The
dome rule stops it showing people's faces while they work.

▌ O24: Visitor Pavilion open nights (2025-08)

Decision: Open nights run on the first and third Friday of each month, weather
permitting, with Corvid on the pavilion terrace. Why: Lena Hofstetter's surveys
found Fridays beat Saturdays for families and Saturdays beat Fridays for
couples; Fridays won by eleven votes.

┌──────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┬───────┬────────────────────┐
│ Item             │ Detail                       │ Owner │ Fallback if cloudy │
