▌ O29: Workshop tool sign-out (2025-09)

Decision: Tools that leave the workshop are signed out on the clipboard by the
door. Why: The torque wrench spent six weeks in the boot of a volunteer's car.

• Hand tools: sign out if leaving the building.
• Power tools: sign out always, even to the car park.
• Optical tools (collimators, the Cheshire eyepiece, the laser): sign out
  always, return the same day.
• The good screwdrivers: ask Tomasz. He will say no. Ask anyway.

▌ O30: Collimation schedule (2025-10)

Decision: Aster is collimated every three months and after any mirror cleaning;
Brill before every outreach season; Corvid never (it is a refractor, and it
would be rude). Why: Aster's star images drifted from round to comet-shaped
between checks when the interval was six months.

▌ O31: The Easter 2025 incident — why raw data stays 14 days (2025-10)

Decision: Raw frames stay on ridge-ctl for 14 days after transfer instead of 7.
Why: Over the Easter weekend of 2025 the archive's incoming disk filled
silently. The end-of-night script's checksum verification failed, but the
failure email went to a mailbox nobody reads over a bank holiday. Seven days
later the cleanup job deleted the originals of two nights that had never been
ingested.

▌ What we changed

1. Cleanup waits 14 days, not 7.
2. Cleanup only removes a night if .transferred exists and stacks confirms the
   ingest.
3. The failure email goes to ops@example.org, which forwards to the on-call
   phone.
4. The archive disk alarm triggers at 80 %, not 95 %.

▌ What we did not change

The two lost nights were mostly cloudy. We are grateful to the cloud, which is
not a sentence anyone at an observatory expects to write.

▎ The rule that came out of it: every automatic deletion must check two
▎ independent facts before it deletes anything.

▌ O32: Emergency contacts board (2025-10)

Decision: A laminated emergency contacts board hangs in the control room, the
workshop and the pavilion. Phone numbers are not reproduced anywhere else in
this handbook. Why: Phone numbers in a document drift out of date and then get
trusted.

▌ O33: Lyra spectrograph scheduling (2025-11)

Decision: Lyra is mounted on Aster only on nights allocated as spectroscopy
nights, at most six per month, and never on open-night Fridays. Why: Swapping
HCAM-2 for Lyra takes 90 minutes and a steady hand. Doing it on a night with
sixty visitors on the terrace has been tried once.

