▌ O44: Supplies we never run out of (2026-03)

Decision: A short list of supplies is checked on the first of each month and
reordered when below the minimum. Why: Running out of lens tissue at 23:00 is a
special kind of sad.

• Lens tissue and optical cleaning fluid (minimum: 2 packs, 1 bottle).
• Red torch batteries (minimum: 40 AA).
• Spare red torches (minimum: 15; they walk).
• Desiccant packs for the filter wheel and HCAM-2 housing (minimum: 10).
• Fuses for the dome controller (minimum: 5 of each rating on the label).
• Coffee (minimum: "enough"; Rosa decides).
• Tea (minimum: also "enough"; Kenji has opinions).
• Hand warmers for winter open nights (minimum: 60 pairs, November to March).

▌ O45: Rebuilding ridge-ctl from scratch (2026-04)

Decision: ridge-ctl can be rebuilt from a blank disk by one person in an
afternoon, using only the ridge-ops repository and the offsite disk. Why: The
control PC is eleven years old. It will die on a clear night; the only question
is which one.

▌ Principles

• Nothing on ridge-ctl is configured by hand that is not also written down in
  ridge-ops.
• Hardware bindings (serial ports, USB IDs) are in udev rules in the repository,
  not in anyone's head.
• The rebuild is rehearsed twice a year on the spare PC in the workshop, which
  is also eleven years old, but differently.

▌ Hardware bindings

The udev rules pin every device to a stable name so that nightjar and halley
never have to guess which /dev/ttyUSB is which after a reboot.

                                                                            ini
 ; /etc/ridge/devices.ini — stable names for hardware on ridge-ctl.
 ; Serial numbers are the real ones on the ridge; the vendor IDs are those of t
↪he adapters we use.
 [mount]
 device  = /dev/ridge-mount
 serial  = HRO-MNT-0042
 baud    = 9600

 [dome]
 device  = /dev/ridge-dome
 serial  = HRO-DOM-0007
 baud    = 19200

 [fw8]
 device  = /dev/ridge-fw8
 serial  = HRO-FW8-0013
 baud    = 9600

 [quill]
 device  = /dev/ridge-quill
 serial  = HRO-QUL-0003
 baud    = 115200
 note    = the Quill head shares a USB hub with the dome controller; if either
↪misbehaves after a storm, reseat the hub first


▌ The rebuild, start to finish

This is the single-paste version. Read it through once before pasting any of it,
and paste it one numbered block at a time.

                                                                             sh
 # 1. Install the base system from the observatory image on the offsite disk.
 #    The image is named hro-base-<YYYY-MM>.img; use the newest one.
 sudo dd if=/media/offsite/images/hro-base-2026-03.img of=/dev/sda bs=4M status
↪=progress conv=fsync

 # 2. After first boot, clone ridge-ops and run the host setup for ridge-ctl.
 git clone ssh://git@ridge-arch.example.invalid/ridge-ops.git /opt/ridge-ops
 cd /opt/ridge-ops && sudo ./hosts/ridge-ctl/setup.sh --with-udev --with-nightj
↪ar --with-halley-client --no-archive

 # 3. Restore the device bindings and reload udev.
 sudo cp /opt/ridge-ops/hosts/ridge-ctl/99-ridge-devices.rules /etc/udev/rules.
↪d/
 sudo udevadm control --reload && sudo udevadm trigger

 # 4. Confirm every device is where it should be.
 for d in mount dome fw8 quill; do test -e /dev/ridge-$d && echo "ok   $d" || e
↪cho "MISSING $d"; done

 # 5. Restore the gateway's known key for ridge-ctl so remote observers are not
↪ warned.
 sudo cp /media/offsite/keys/ridge-ctl/ssh_host_ed25519_key* /etc/ssh/ && sudo
↪systemctl restart ssh

 # 6. Dry run: home everything, open nothing.
 nightjar selftest --dry-run --no-dome


▌ After the rebuild

1. Take one set of bias frames and compare the median level with the last good
   night.
2. Point Aster at a bright star with the dome shut (it is fine; the dome does
   not mind) and check the mount reports sane coordinates.
3. Log the rebuild in the night log and in ridge-ops/hosts/ridge-ctl/HISTORY.md.

▌ O46: Brill in the East Roll-off (2026-04)

Decision: Brill's roll-off roof opens and closes by hand only. It is not
connected to the Sable interlock. Why: The roll-off roof motor was removed in
2019 after it tried to close on the telescope. A roof that only moves when a
human pushes it cannot surprise that human.

Consequences:

• Brill is never left observing unattended.
• The roll-off has its own copy of the O6 weather table taped inside the door.
• Anyone observing with Brill checks Sable on the roll-off tablet every 30
  minutes.

▌ O47: Corvid lives indoors (2026-04)

Decision: Corvid is stored in the pavilion and carried to the terrace for each
open night. Why: It weighs 14 kg with its mount and is the one telescope
volunteers can safely carry. Left outdoors, it would weigh 14 kg plus the local
spider population.

▌ O48: Nightjar priorities (2026-05)

Decision: nightjar resolves conflicts between observing blocks by priority
number, then by cadence urgency, then by altitude.

┌──────────┬──────────────────────────────────────┬──────────────┐
│ Priority │ Who uses it                          │ Can pre-empt │
╞══════════╪══════════════════════════════════════╪══════════════╡
│ 0        │ Targets of opportunity (novae, etc.) │ everything   │
│ 1        │ Allocated visiting astronomer time   │ 2, 3, 4      │
│ 2        │ VSS fast and calibration cadences    │ 3, 4         │
│ 3        │ VSS medium and slow cadences         │ 4            │
│ 4        │ Engineering, tests, pretty pictures  │ nothing      │
└──────────┴──────────────────────────────────────┴──────────────┘

Why: A bright nova appeared in March 2026 during a visiting astronomer's
allocation. The astronomer was delighted to be pre-empted. Not everyone will be,
which is why priority 0 needs the director's or the night assistant's approval
and a line in the night log.

▎ Targets of opportunity are rare. When one arrives, write down everything: the
▎ time you heard, who told you, what you did first. Future you will want the
▎ timeline.

▌ O49: Photometry quality flags (2026-05)

Decision: halley assigns every VSS measurement a quality flag, and only flags A
and B go into the published light curves.

┌──────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬───────────┐
│ Flag │ Meaning                                             │ Published │
╞══════╪═════════════════════════════════════════════════════╪═══════════╡
│ A    │ Seeing < 2.5", airmass < 1.5, no warnings           │ yes       │
│ B    │ Seeing < 3.5" or airmass < 2.0, no warnings         │ yes       │
│ C    │ Anything with a halley warning (saturation, cosmic) │ no        │
│ D    │ Calibration missing or older than seven days        │ no        │
│ X    │ Manually rejected by a reviewer                     │ no        │
└──────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴───────────┘

Why: Early light curves mixed good and marginal points and looked noisier than
the stars really are. Kenji put it more politely.

▌ O50: Public data releases (2026-06)

Decision: VSS light curves are released publicly twice a year, in January and
July, with a short release note. Why: Continuous release sounded good until we
had to explain why a point moved between two downloads.

Each release note covers:

• The halley version used (O18).
• How many stars and measurements are included, by quality flag.
• Any stars dropped or added, with reasons.
• Known issues, stated plainly.
• How to cite the release.

▌ O51: Cleaning the Aster primary (2026-06)

Decision: Aster's primary mirror is washed at most once a year, in June, when
the nights are short anyway. Between washes it is blown with clean dry air only.
Why: Every wash risks the coating more than a little dust costs in throughput.
The June wash also lines up with the O30 collimation that must follow it.

▌ The wash, in outline

1. Book two people and a full day in the workshop.
2. Remove the mirror cell following runbooks/remove-aster-primary.md.
3. Rinse with distilled water, then the mild detergent solution, then distilled
   water again.
4. Drain on edge, never wipe.
5. Reinstall, collimate (O30), take a test frame, compare throughput with last
   season.

▎ "Never wipe" is in bold in the runbook, underlined on the workshop whiteboard,
▎ and was once embroidered onto a tea towel by a volunteer. It remains good
▎ advice.

▌ O52: Kenji's remote observing hours (2026-07)

Decision: Remote observers in time zones far from the ridge get a night
assistant on site but may run their own blocks from home, through the gateway
(O22). Why: Kenji observes from a timezone where the ridge's midnight is his
breakfast. He is sharper at breakfast than we are at midnight, and he knows his
targets better than anyone here.

▌ O53: What a quiet night looks like in the logs (2026-07)

Decision: None. This entry exists so new night assistants know what normal looks
like.

