↪d verified


▌ O54: Handbook review cadence (2026-08)

Decision: The whole handbook is read by at least two staff each September,
before the season starts, and every entry is marked still-true, needs-update, or
superseded. Why: A handbook that is not re-read becomes the old wiki page titled
"Stuff".

• Ada reads the odd-numbered entries.
• Tomasz reads the even-numbered entries.
• Anyone else is welcome, and usually finds the most.
• Disagreements go on the agenda of the first Monday meeting of the season.

▌ O55: Where this handbook lives (2026-09)

Decision: This handbook lives in the ridge-ops repository beside the runbooks,
and a copy is printed and kept in a ring binder in the control room. Why: The
control room binder works when ridge-arch does not, which is exactly when you
need it most. It is reprinted after each September review (O54).

The public version, with access details and internal addresses removed, is
published at https://example.org/hro/handbook for other small observatories to
borrow from. Borrow freely; tell us what you changed.

▌ O56: Monday meeting format (2026-09)

Decision: The Monday meeting runs for 45 minutes, in the pavilion, with a fixed
agenda, and ends on time even if the agenda does not. Why: The old Monday
meeting had no agenda, ran for two hours, and once spent forty minutes on the
colour of the new pavilion bins.

▌ The agenda

1. Last week's nights: clear, cloudy, lost, and why (5 minutes, Rosa).
2. Faults and incidents opened or closed (10 minutes, Tomasz).
3. Data: ingests, backlogs, anything stuck in incoming/ (5 minutes, Priya).
4. Outreach: bookings, open nights, school visits (5 minutes, Lena).
5. Handbook: any entry proposed, changed or superseded this week (10 minutes,
   Ada).
6. Anything else (10 minutes, strictly).

▌ Minutes

Minutes are written during the meeting by whoever is not speaking, rotated
weekly, and filed as logs/<YYYY-MM-DD>-monday.md. They record decisions and
owners, not discussion.

▎ If a decision in the minutes changes how the observatory runs, it is not
▎ finished until it has an O-number in this handbook.

▌ O57: School visits (2026-09)

Decision: School groups visit on weekday afternoons in term time, see the Main
Dome with the slit open and the telescope parked, and look at the Sun through
the pavilion's filtered solar telescope, which is the only telescope on the
ridge allowed to point at the Sun. Why: Children are much more excited by a
moving dome than by a slideshow, and a daytime visit does not need a bus home at
midnight.

┌──────────┬──────────┬────────┬────────────────────────────┬──────────────────┐
│ Age      │ Group    │ Length │ Content                    │ Staff            │
│ group    │ size     │        │                            │                  │
╞══════════╪══════════╪════════╪════════════════════════════╪══════════════════╡
│ 7–9      │ up to 25 │ 90 min │ Dome, solar telescope,     │ Lena + 1         │
│          │          │        │ "how big is the Moon"      │ volunteer        │
├──────────┼──────────┼────────┼────────────────────────────┼──────────────────┤
│ 10–13    │ up to 30 │ 2 h    │ Dome, solar telescope,     │ Lena + 2         │
│          │          │        │ Tern meteor examples       │ volunteers       │
├──────────┼──────────┼────────┼────────────────────────────┼──────────────────┤
│ 14–18    │ up to 30 │ 2.5 h  │ All of the above, plus a   │ Lena + Priya or  │
│          │          │        │ real VSS light curve       │ Min-jun          │
└──────────┴──────────┴────────┴────────────────────────────┴──────────────────┘

The solar telescope has its own rules:

• It is set up only by Lena or a trained volunteer.
• The filter is inspected for pinholes before every session, against a bright
  lamp, indoors.
• It is never left unattended while pointed at the Sun, not even "just for a
  second".
• Nobody improvises a solar filter for any other telescope. Ever.

▎ The pavilion finder scopes all have their caps glued on for school visits. It
▎ looks untidy. It is on purpose.

▌ O58: Accessibility on open nights (2026-09)

Decision: Every open night has at least one telescope with an eyepiece reachable
from a seated position, and the terrace path is kept clear and lit with low red
strip lights. Why: Lena's visitor survey found that one visitor in twelve had
difficulty with the Corvid eyepiece at standing height, and several simply left
rather than ask.

• Corvid's tripod is set to its lowest height for the first half hour of every
  open night.
• A second eyepiece on a flexible extension is available on request.
• Brill's roll-off has a ramp, not steps, since the 2025 rebuild.
• Live images from Brill are shown on the pavilion screen for anyone who prefers
  to sit indoors.
• Booking asks, gently and optionally, whether a visitor needs any of the above.

▌ O59: Data retention (2026-09)

Decision: How long each kind of data is kept, in one place. Why: The question
came up at three different Monday meetings and got three different answers.

