│ Night assistant │ The person who owns the night (O2)                         │
├─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Standing plan   │ What Aster observes when nothing else is scheduled (O15)   │
├─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Target of       │ Something unexpected and bright enough to pre-empt the     │
│ opportunity     │ plan (O48)                                                 │
└─────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

▌ Abbreviations

• HRO: Halden Ridge Observatory.
• VSS: the Variable Star Survey.
• UPS: uninterruptible power supply (O37).
• UTC: the only time zone this handbook uses (O3).
• TOO: target of opportunity.
• RC: Ritchey–Chrétien, Aster's optical design.
• SCT: Schmidt–Cassegrain telescope, Brill's optical design.
• FITS: the image file format every frame is saved in.

Abbreviations for people in the night log are initials only: RD, MS, TW, PR, LH,
KM, YH, AO. A new person picks initials that do not clash with anyone already
listed in ridge-ops/training/ (O60); if they do clash, the newer person adds a
digit, and is teased about it for exactly one week.

▌ Where to ask

• Anything about the night: the night assistant on shift.
• Anything about hardware: Tomasz, or the fault log in ridge-ops/faults/.
• Anything about data: Priya, or ops@example.org.
• Anything about visitors: Lena.
• Anything about this handbook: Ada, or bring it to the Monday meeting (O56).
• Anything about biscuits: Rosa, who will deny everything.
• Anything else: the Monday meeting, which exists for exactly this.

If you are reading this in the control room binder at night and none of the
above can be reached, the rule is the same as ever: close the dome, park the
telescope, and write down what happened."
`;

exports[`snapshots of the layout IR as text handbook/writing-guide.md @ 80 1`] = `
"── ≡ properties ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 title        Writing guide for notes, logs and reports
 description  How Halden Ridge Observatory writes its night logs, working notes
              and monthly reports.
 tags         handbook, writing, logs, style
 aliases      style guide, how to write a night log
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

▌ Writing guide

This guide covers everything we write down at Halden Ridge: night logs, working
notes, incident reports and the monthly report to the trust. It is maintained by
Ada Okafor, with long-suffering contributions from Priya Raman (who reads every
log the pipeline chokes on) and Rosa Delgado (who writes most of them at three
in the morning).

The short version: write for the person who opens this note in two years, knows
nothing about tonight, and is trying to work out why frame 4417 of the Variable
Star Survey looks wrong. That person is usually you.

▌ Why we bother

A log that says "clouds, closed" is not a log. It is a shrug with a timestamp.
The archive in stacks keeps every frame we have ever taken, but it cannot tell
anyone why the dome closed at 23:40, which filter was stuck, or that the Sable
weather station had been reading two degrees warm since the Workshop door was
left open. Only the notes can. When the Kiso-Hara Astronomical Society asked for
our 2025 photometry of the eclipsing binaries, Kenji Mori spent a week
reconstructing three nights because the logs said "some issue with FW-8" and
nothing more; that week is the reason this guide exists.

▌ Naming notes

▌ File names

• Use lower-case words joined by hyphens: dunmore-mirror-cell.md, not Dunmore
  Mirror Cell (NEW).md.
• Put the date first for anything that happens on a day:
  2026-03-14-night-log.md.
• Never put a person's name in a file name; people move on, notes stay.
• Avoid version suffixes like -final or -v2; the history lives in git, not in
  the name.
• Keep names under 50 characters so they fit in the sidebar without truncating.

▌ Titles

• The first heading is the title and matches the title field in the frontmatter.
• A title says what the note is about, not what kind of note it is: "Dunmore
  secondary mirror spider" beats "Notes (Dunmore)".
• For decisions, phrase the title as the decision: "Move Tern to the pavilion
  roof".

▌ Folders

┌────────────┬────────────────────────────────────┬──────────────┬─────────────┐
│ Folder     │ What goes in it                    │ Who owns it  │ Review      │
│            │                                    │              │ cadence     │
╞════════════╪════════════════════════════════════╪══════════════╪═════════════╡
│ logs/      │ Night logs, one per observing      │ Night        │ Weekly      │
│            │ night                              │ assistant    │             │
├────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────┼─────────────┤
│ handbook/  │ Procedures, this guide, runbooks   │ Director     │ Quarterly   │
├────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────┼─────────────┤
│ projects/  │ Project notes: VSS, Dunmore,       │ Project lead │ Monthly     │
│            │ Meteor                             │              │             │
├────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────┼─────────────┤
│ incidents/ │ Anything that broke or nearly      │ Chief        │ After each  │
│            │ broke                              │ engineer     │             │
├────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────┼─────────────┤
│ outreach/  │ Visitor nights, school talks,      │ Outreach     │ Monthly     │
│            │ posters                            │ lead         │             │
├────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────┼─────────────┤
│ reports/   │ Monthly reports to the trust       │ Director     │ Monthly     │
├────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────┼─────────────┤
│ scratch/   │ Half-formed thoughts, allowed to   │ Whoever      │ Never       │
│            │ rot                                │ wrote it     │             │
└────────────┴────────────────────────────────────┴──────────────┴─────────────┘

▌ Headings

• One # heading per note: the title.
• Use ## for the main sections and ### for subsections; stop there. If you need
  a fourth level, the note wants to be two notes.
• Headings are nouns or short phrases, not sentences: "Focus run", not "We then
  ran the focus routine".
• A night log always has the same sections in the same order, so a search for ##
  Weather finds every one.

▌ Night logs

▌ Required sections

1. Summary: two or three lines. Was the night usable, and for what?
2. Weather: numbers from Sable and Quill, plus what you saw with your own eyes.
3. Observations: the table described below.
4. Problems: anything that failed, with times.
5. Handover: what the next person needs to know before opening the dome.

▌ A good entry

                                                                             md
 ## Problems

 - 22:14 FW-8 stuck between V and R during slot change. Dome lights on, wheel c
↪licked twice, then moved.
   Frames 4411–4416 (all V) are fine; 4417 was taken mid-move and is marked \`ba
↪d\` in stacks.
 - 22:30 Re-homed FW-8 from \`ridge-ctl\` (\`fw8 home\`), no further sticking tonig
↪ht.
 - Told Tomasz by message; he wants the wheel's belt tension checked before Fri
↪day.


▌ A bad entry

                                                                             md
 ## Problems

 - filter wheel weird again
 - some frames bad??
 - fixed it


The bad entry is not wrong, exactly. It is just useless: which wheel, which
frames, how was it fixed, and does anyone need to do anything? Every one of
those questions costs someone twenty minutes later.

▌ Another good entry, for a quiet night

                                                                             md
 ## Summary

 Clear from 20:50 to 04:10, seeing 1.4–1.9" on Quill. Full VSS block A (38 targ
↪ets) on Aster with HCAM-2.
 Nothing broke. Closed at 04:12 on humidity (Sable 91%). No handover items.


▌ Tables for observations

Every night log has one observation table. Columns are fixed so that halley can
parse them; do not rename or reorder them.

┌────────────┬─────────────┬─────────┬──────────────┐
│ Time (UTC) │ Target      │ Filter  │ Exposure (s) │
╞════════════╪═════════════╪═════════╪══════════════╡
│ 20:58      │ V0412 Cyg   │ V       │ 60           │
│ 21:03      │ V0412 Cyg   │ R       │ 60           │
│ 21:11      │ RR Lyr      │ V       │ 30           │
│ 21:15      │ RR Lyr      │ B       │ 45           │
│ 21:24      │ SW And      │ V       │ 90           │
│ 21:31      │ SW And      │ I       │ 90           │
│ 21:40      │ Flat, dusk  │ clear   │ 2            │
│ 22:05      │ Standard 14 │ V       │ 20           │
│ 22:09      │ Standard 14 │ B       │ 30           │
│ 22:14      │ Standard 14 │ R       │ 20           │
│ 22:31      │ UV Boo      │ V       │ 120          │
│ 22:48      │ UV Boo      │ H-alpha │ 300          │
│ 23:20      │ Dark        │ —       │ 120          │
│ 23:27      │ Bias        │ —       │ 0            │
│ 23:35      │ M 57        │ OIII    │ 600          │
│ 23:49      │ M 57        │ H-alpha │ 600          │
│ 00:02      │ Dark        │ —       │ 600          │
└────────────┴─────────────┴─────────┴──────────────┘

• Times are UTC, always. The pavilion clock is local time and has been wrong
  since the power cut in April 2025.
• One row per exposure series, not per frame; the frame numbers live in stacks.
• Use the target names from the VSS target list, exactly as spelled there.
• Use an em dash (—) for "not applicable", never an empty cell.

▌ Units

• Seeing in arcseconds with the " symbol: 1.6". Quill reports FWHM; say so if
  you quote something else.
• Temperatures in °C, humidity in %, wind in m/s, pressure in hPa.
• Exposure times in seconds, no unit in the table (the header says it), unit in
  prose: "a 300 s exposure".
• Magnitudes to two decimals unless the pipeline gives more and you mean it.
• Mirror and dome dimensions in metres or millimetres, never inches, even for
  Dunmore, whose original drawings are all in inches and are a source of ongoing
  sorrow for Tomasz.

▌ Citing instruments and software

When you write about a measurement, name the instrument that made it and, if it
matters, its configuration. Write "HCAM-2 on Aster, binned 2×2, gain 1.2 e⁻/ADU,
FW-8 slot 3 (R)" rather than "the camera", because in 2027 there may be an
HCAM-3 and nobody will remember which one "the camera" meant; the same goes for
software, so write "halley 4.2.1, nightjar schedule vss-a-2026-03" and not "the
pipeline", and when a result depends on a calibration frame, give its stacks id
so anyone can pull it back out.

• Instrument names are proper nouns: Aster, Brill, Corvid, Dunmore, Lyra, Quill,
  Sable, Tern.
• Product-style codes keep their capitals and hyphen: HCAM-2, FW-8.
• Software names are lower-case and in backticks when you mean the command:
  halley, nightjar, stacks.
• Hosts are always in backticks: ridge-ctl, ridge-arch, ridge-web.

▌ Links

• Link to other notes with relative links: see the operations handbook for dome
  procedures and the Dunmore project page for the mirror work.
• Link to the incident note from the night log, and from the incident back to
  the log, as in the FW-8 incident.
• The public calendar is at https://halden-ridge.example.org/calendar; the
  internal wiki lives at https://wiki.example.invalid/hro and is not reachable
  from outside the ridge.
• Style questions go to the monthly report template or to the glossary before
  they go to Ada.

▌ Incident reports

▌ Structure

1. What happened, with times in UTC.
2. What we saw first, and who saw it.
3. What we did, in order, including the things that did not work.
4. What it cost: lost hours, lost frames, damaged hardware.
5. What changes, with an owner for each change.

Items 1–5 are the shared template; incidents that touched hardware carry two
more, numbered on from where the template stops (the gap is the template's
sixth, "sign-off", which lives in the header):

7. The part, by name and serial number, and where it is now.
8. Whether the vendor has been told, and the reference they gave.

Before item 1 comes a step nobody skips, numbered zero so that it cannot be
mistaken for part of the template:

0. Make the hardware safe: dome closed, telescope parked, power isolated if
   anything smells.
1. Then, and only then, open the note.

When a log refers to frames by number, a numbered list can use the frame numbers
directly. The night of 2026-03-14 crossed a thousand frames during block B:

 998. V, 60 s, variable target VSS-0117, fine.
 999. V, 60 s, the same target, a satellite trail across the check star.
1000. R, 60 s, the wheel stopped mid-slot here (the FW-8 incident).
1001. R, 60 s, after fw8 home, fine again.

▌ Example

                                                                             md
 # 2026-03-14 FW-8 stuck mid-slot

 - 22:14 UTC: wheel stopped between V and R (Rosa, night log).
 - 22:16: soft reset from nightjar failed; wheel reported "in position".
 - 22:30: \`fw8 home\` on ridge-ctl recovered it.
 - Cost: one frame (4417), 16 minutes of VSS block A.
 - Change: check belt tension monthly (owner: Tomasz). Add a position check to
↪nightjar before each exposure
   (owner: Priya).


▌ Checking notes before you commit

The script below lives in tools/ on ridge-ctl and checks that every night log
has the required sections, that times look like UTC, and that nobody has put a
real password in a note (it has happened once).

                                                                           bash
 #!/usr/bin/env bash
 # check-notes.sh: sanity checks for night logs before they go into the vault
 set -euo pipefail

 vault="\${1:-$HOME/vault}"
 required=("## Summary" "## Weather" "## Observations" "## Problems" "## Handov
↪er")
 status=0

 for log in "$vault"/logs/*-night-log.md; do
   for section in "\${required[@]}"; do
     if ! grep -qxF "$section" "$log"; then
       echo "missing '$section' in $(basename "$log")"; status=1
     fi
   done
   if grep -nE '\\b(password|passwd|secret)\\s*[:=]' "$log"; then
     echo "possible secret in $(basename "$log"), remove it and tell Tomasz"; s
↪tatus=1
   fi
 done

 find "$vault" -name '*.md' -newer "$vault/.last-check" -print0 | xargs -0 -r g
↪rep -nE '^\\| [0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2} +\\|' | awk -F'|' '$2 !~ /^ [0-2][0-9]:[0-5][0-9
↪] +$/ { print "odd time: " $0 }'
 touch "$vault/.last-check"
 exit "$status"


Run it like this before pushing:

                                                                           bash
 cd ~/vault && ./tools/check-notes.sh . && git add logs/ && git commit -m "Nigh
↪t log $(date -u +%F)"


▌ HTML in notes

Markdown covers almost everything. Where it does not, a few HTML tags are
allowed, and nothing else: keep them for things the reader would otherwise miss.

• A forced line break inside a table cell or list item: Aster<br>Main Dome,
  which renders as Aster
  Main Dome.
• Keys, when a procedure says what to press: save the plan with Ctrl+S.
• Powers and chemistry: the flat field falls off as r2 near the edge, and the
  dome dehumidifier removes about 3 litres of H2O on a damp night.

For a long justification that most readers can skip, fold it:

<details><summary>Why</summary>
Night logs are read on the control room screen at 03:00 by someone tired. A fold
ed paragraph keeps
the log short for them and complete for the person who opens it in two years.
</details>

▌ Monthly reports

• Keep the report to two pages when printed; the trust reads it on paper at the
  Marrowby library.
• Start with three numbers: clear hours, usable hours, frames archived.
• One paragraph per project: VSS, Dunmore Refurbishment, Dark Sky Campaign,
  Meteor Watch.
• Put the outreach numbers (visitors, school groups, Corvid sessions) in a short
  list, not a table.

Lena Hofstetter writes the outreach section and has asked, politely and
repeatedly, that the rest of us stop describing visitor nights as "busy" and
start writing down how many people came, how many of them looked through Corvid,
how many were children, and whether the Dark Sky Campaign leaflets ran out,
because the council meeting in June wants numbers and "busy" does not survive a
council meeting.

▌ Checklist before closing a note

• The frontmatter has a title, description and tags.
• The title matches the first heading.
• All times are UTC and say so once.
• Every instrument and host is named, not "the camera" or "the server".
• Units are present and consistent.
• Bad frames are marked in stacks and mentioned in the note.
• Problems have an owner, or explicitly say "no action".
• Links to related notes work (click them once).
• The check script passes.

▌ See also

• Operations handbook
• Glossary"
`;

exports[`snapshots of the layout IR as text home.md @ 80 1`] = `
"▌ Halden Ridge Observatory

Welcome to the observatory's notes: runbooks, logs, plans and reference, all
linked. Everything here is fictional — this vault is slipbox's demo, and
about-this-vault says what each note is there to exercise.

▌ Start here

• operations-handbook — how the observatory runs, one decision at a time
• writing-guide — how we write notes, logs and reports
• variable-star-survey — the survey's planning document
• dome-refit — the summer shutdown's dome shutter refit

▌ Equipment

• telescope-comparison — Aster, Brill, Corvid, Dunmore, and what might replace
  them
• instrument-inventory — cameras, spectrograph, weather, hosts

▌ Logs and software

• observing-log-2026 — every night of 2026 so far
• pipeline-cookbook — recipes for halley and nightjar

▌ People and meetings

• partners-and-names — partners, and names in their own scripts
• 2026-09-14-weekly — the last weekly meeting
• kenji-mori — our visiting astronomer
• contacts — two notes are called contacts, people/contacts.md and
  equipment/contacts.md: a path names one

▌ Outreach

• outreach — the public programme: a vault of its own, nested in this one
  (outreach/)"
`;

exports[`snapshots of the layout IR as text logs/observing-log-2026.md @ 80 1`] = `
"▌ Observing log 2026

Night-by-night record for Halden Ridge Observatory. Times are UTC. Weather
readings come from Sable, seeing from Quill (arcsec, FWHM), sky brightness in
mag/arcsec². Faults get a ticket number in the Workshop tracker. The year opened
on the first night; every night's weather is under its own ### Weather.

The rule for this page is simple: one heading per night, one table per heading,
and if a night is lost to cloud it still gets a row.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

▌ 2026-01-04 — first light of the year

┌───────┬─────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Time  │ Target / Event  │ Notes                                              │
╞═══════╪═════════════════╪════════════════════════════════════════════════════╡
│ 17:42 │ Dome open       │ 2.1 °C, RH 64 %, wind 3 m/s NW                     │
├───────┼─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 17:55 │ Flats, twilight │ FW-8 B V R, 7 frames each                          │
├───────┼─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 18:40 │ RZ Cas          │ VSS, HCAM-2, V, 60 s × 40                          │
├───────┼─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 20:15 │ Quill           │ seeing 1.8″, steady                                │
├───────┼─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 21:02 │ SS Cyg          │ quiescent, V ≈ 12.1                                │
├───────┼─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 23:30 │ Dome closed     │ cloud from the west, Tern shows 60 % cover; 312    │
│       │                 │ frames kept, none of them flats                    │
└───────┴─────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

▌ Weather

Clear after sunset, cloud from the west by 23:00. Sable: 2.1 °C, RH 64 %.

▌ 2026-01-11 — Brill test night

┌───────┬─────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Time  │ Target / Event  │ Notes                                           │
╞═══════╪═════════════════╪═════════════════════════════════════════════════╡
│ 18:05 │ Roll-off open   │ roof motor loud on the last metre               │
│ 18:20 │ Focus run       │ Brill, HCAM-2 borrowed, best at 14 220 steps    │
│ 19:10 │ M42             │ outreach test frames for Lena, H-alpha 120 s    │
│ 20:00 │ Fault           │ roof limit switch bounced twice — ticket W-0112 │
│ 20:45 │ Sable           │ 0.4 °C, RH 71 %                                 │
│ 22:10 │ Roll-off closed │ dew on the corrector, heater was off            │
└───────┴─────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

▌ Weather

Cold and still. Sable: −3.4 °C, RH 51 %, wind under 2 m/s all night.

▌ 2026-01-17 — lost to cloud

┌───────┬─────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Time  │ Target / Event  │ Notes                                              │
╞═══════╪═════════════════╪════════════════════════════════════════════════════╡
│ 17:30 │ Check           │ overcast, base ~900 m                              │
├───────┼─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 19:00 │ Check           │ still overcast, light drizzle                      │
├───────┼─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 21:00 │ Sable           │ RH 98 %, rain sensor wet                           │
├───────┼─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 21:05 │ Night abandoned │ nightjar queue flushed to tomorrow                 │
├───────┼─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 21:20 │ Server Room     │ used the time to rotate stacks backups and test a  │
│       │                 │ restore to a scratch database                      │
└───────┴─────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

▌ Weather

Overcast from dusk. Tern never showed a star; the dome stayed shut.

▌ 2026-01-24 — VSS catch-up

┌───────┬────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Time  │ Target / Event │ Notes                                               │
╞═══════╪════════════════╪═════════════════════════════════════════════════════╡
│ 17:48 │ Dome open      │ −3.5 °C, clear, wind 1 m/s                          │
├───────┼────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 18:02 │ Bias + darks   │ 21 each, CCD at −40 °C                              │
├───────┼────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 18:30 │ VSS field 07   │ 11 stars, V and I, 45 s                             │
├───────┼────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 19:55 │ VSS field 08   │ 9 stars, one saturated (BR-0831), shorten to 20 s   │
│       │                │ next time                                           │
├───────┼────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 21:10 │ VSS field 12   │ 14 stars, all fine                                  │
├───────┼────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 22:40 │ Quill          │ 1.3″ — best of the month                            │
├───────┼────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 01:15 │ VSS field 15   │ halley flags airmass > 2.2, kept anyway             │
├───────┼────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 03:05 │ Dome closed    │ 2 612 frames ingested, 0 rejected                   │
└───────┴────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

▌ Weather

Clear, a little haze low in the south. Quill averaged 2.0″.

▌ 2026-02-02 — Lyra commissioning, night 1

┌───────┬────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Time  │ Target / Event │ Notes                                               │
╞═══════╪════════════════╪═════════════════════════════════════════════════════╡
│ 18:10 │ Lyra on Aster  │ Kenji on site, cable routing done in daylight       │
├───────┼────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 18:40 │ Arc lamp       │ Ne + Ar, 12 lines identified                        │
├───────┼────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 19:25 │ Standard star  │ spectrophotometric standard, 300 s                  │
├───────┼────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 20:30 │ Fault          │ Lyra slit drive stalls at 180 µm; motor current     │
│       │                │ normal, so mechanical rather than electrical        │
├───────┼────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 21:05 │ Workaround     │ fixed slit at 150 µm for the night                  │
├───────┼────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 23:50 │ Be star        │ Hα emission obvious in raw frame                    │
├───────┼────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 00:30 │ Dome closed    │ Kenji happy, Tomasz less so                         │
└───────┴────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

▌ 2026-02-03 — Lyra commissioning, night 2

┌───────┬────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Time  │ Target / Event │ Notes                                    │
╞═══════╪════════════════╪══════════════════════════════════════════╡
│ 17:58 │ Dome open      │ 1.2 °C, RH 55 %                          │
│ 18:20 │ Slit drive     │ re-greased by Tomasz, runs full range    │
│ 19:00 │ Flat lamp      │ 20 flats, fringing visible past 780 nm   │
│ 20:10 │ Nova candidate │ Kenji's list, nothing unusual            │
│ 22:45 │ Sable          │ wind rising, 11 m/s gusts                │
│ 23:00 │ Dome closed    │ wind limit is 12 m/s; didn't wait for it │
└───────┴────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────┘

▌ 2026-02-09 — public night

┌───────┬─────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Time  │ Target / Event  │ Notes                                              │
╞═══════╪═════════════════╪════════════════════════════════════════════════════╡
│ 18:30 │ Pavilion open   │ 46 visitors booked, 51 came                        │
