exports[`snapshots of the layout IR as text projects/variable-star-survey.md @ 80 1`] = `
"▌ Variable Star Survey — planning document

Working plan for the Variable Star Survey (VSS) at Halden Ridge Observatory.
This is the long, messy, living version: threads of work, the options we looked
at, what we decided and what is still open. The tidy summary for the trust board
lives elsewhere; this one keeps the arguments.

Owners, roughly:

• Ada Okafor — survey lead, final call on scope.
• Priya Raman — halley pipeline, photometry, archive schema.
• Tomasz Wierzbicki — Aster, HCAM-2, FW-8, anything with a screwdriver.
• Rosa Delgado — night operations, nightjar queue hygiene.
• Kenji Mori — visiting, comparison-star sequences and the period analysis.
• Min-jun Seo — intern, target list, finding charts, a lot of spreadsheet
  archaeology.

Related notes: operations-handbook for the night routine, telescope-comparison
for why Aster does the survey and Brill does the follow-up, and
pipeline-cookbook for the halley recipes referenced below.

Status as of 2026-03-18: threads 1 and 2 are mostly decided, thread 3 is in
pilot, thread 4 is half-built, thread 5 is a list of worries. 🔭

▎ The survey is not the telescope and it is not the pipeline. It is the list,
▎ and whether we keep observing the list for five years without getting bored.
▎
▎ — Ada, kickoff meeting, 2025-11-04

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

▌ Thread 1 — Target selection

The first thing everyone wanted to argue about was which stars. We started with
a scraped list of about 1,900 candidates from three public catalogues and a pile
of amateur reports, and we need to get down to roughly 400 that Aster can reach,
that are bright enough for HCAM-2 in V and I, and that actually vary on
timescales we can sample from one ridge. ^thread-one

▌ Why a fixed list and not a rolling one

We looked at three ways of running the target list:

1. Fixed list — choose ~400 stars once, observe them for the life of the survey,
   add nothing without a board vote.
2. Rolling list — keep a core of ~250 and rotate the remaining slots every
   season based on what looks interesting.
3. Alert-driven — a small core plus whatever the outside alert streams flag each
   week.

The rolling list sounded clever and would have been miserable: every rotation
means new comparison stars, new finding charts and a fresh round of "why is this
one noisy". Alerts are fun, and they are what Brill is for.

▎ If we rotate, in three years we have 900 stars with eighteen months of data
▎ each and not one of them tells a story.
▎
▎ — Kenji, target meeting, 2025-11-19

Decision: fixed list, with a reserve of 40 "bench" stars that can be promoted
only when a core star is dropped for a documented reason (too faint after all, a
bright neighbour, a blend we missed).

▌ Cuts, in order

Min-jun applied the cuts in this order, which matters because the counts depend
on it:

┌──────┬───────────────────────────┬───────────┬───────────────────────────────┐
│ Step │ Cut                       │ Remaining │ Notes                         │
╞══════╪═══════════════════════════╪═══════════╪═══════════════════════════════╡
│ 0    │ Merged candidate list     │     1,912 │ duplicates resolved by        │
│      │                           │           │ position within 3″            │
├──────┼───────────────────────────┼───────────┼───────────────────────────────┤
│ 1    │ Declination > −25°        │     1,406 │ Aster below 20° altitude is   │
│      │                           │           │ not worth it                  │
├──────┼───────────────────────────┼───────────┼───────────────────────────────┤
│ 2    │ 8.5 < V < 15.5 at maximum │       988 │ saturation at the bright end, │
│      │                           │           │ SNR at the faint              │
├──────┼───────────────────────────┼───────────┼───────────────────────────────┤
│ 3    │ No neighbour within 12″   │       731 │ blends are the silent killer  │
│      │ brighter than it          │           │                               │
├──────┼───────────────────────────┼───────────┼───────────────────────────────┤
│ 4    │ Known period 0.2 d – 400  │       602 │ we cannot sample faster than  │
│      │ d, or unknown             │           │ ~20 min cadence               │
├──────┼───────────────────────────┼───────────┼───────────────────────────────┤
│ 5    │ Amplitude > 0.1 mag       │       447 │ smaller is Lyra's problem,    │
│      │                           │           │ not ours                      │
├──────┼───────────────────────────┼───────────┼───────────────────────────────┤
│ 6    │ Hand review of finding    │       412 │ Min-jun and Kenji, two        │
│      │ charts                    │           │ weekends, much tea            │
└──────┴───────────────────────────┴───────────┴───────────────────────────────┘

The final 412 split as 400 core plus 12 that went straight onto the bench
because the charts looked suspicious.

▌ Classes in the core list

┌──────────────────┬───────┬────────────┬───────────────────┬──────────────────┐
│ Class            │ Count │ Typical    │ Typical amplitude │ Cadence we want  │
│                  │       │ period     │               (V) │                  │
╞══════════════════╪═══════╪════════════╪═══════════════════╪══════════════════╡
│ Mira /           │    96 │ 150–400 d  │         2.5–6 mag │ every 5–7 nights │
│ long-period      │       │            │                   │                  │
├──────────────────┼───────┼────────────┼───────────────────┼──────────────────┤
│ Semi-regular     │   112 │ 30–150 d   │       0.3–1.5 mag │ every 3–4 nights │
├──────────────────┼───────┼────────────┼───────────────────┼──────────────────┤
│ Eclipsing binary │    84 │ 0.3–10 d   │       0.2–1.2 mag │ dense runs at    │
│                  │       │            │                   │ eclipse          │
├──────────────────┼───────┼────────────┼───────────────────┼──────────────────┤
│ Cepheid          │    31 │ 2–40 d     │       0.4–1.2 mag │ nightly          │
├──────────────────┼───────┼────────────┼───────────────────┼──────────────────┤
│ RR Lyrae         │    44 │ 0.3–0.9 d  │       0.5–1.1 mag │ 20-min runs, 3 h │
├──────────────────┼───────┼────────────┼───────────────────┼──────────────────┤
│ Unknown /        │    33 │ ?          │          ≥0.1 mag │ nightly until we │
│ unclassified     │       │            │                   │ know             │
└──────────────────┴───────┴────────────┴───────────────────┴──────────────────┘

▌ The unclassified 33

These are the ones Kenji cares about most and the ones most likely to be wrong.
Several are probably blends that step 3 missed because the neighbour is faint in
V and bright in I. Rule: any unclassified star that has not shown variability
above 0.1 mag after 60 usable nights gets moved to the bench, no discussion.

▌ Open questions for thread 1

• Do we keep the three stars that are within 40′ of the Moon's path for a week a
  month, or accept the gaps?
• Is 15.5 at maximum really reachable in I with HCAM-2 in a 120 s exposure at
  our sky brightness? Tomasz thinks so, Priya's first test frames say "barely".
• Who owns the bench? Currently "Min-jun, until September", which is not a plan.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

▌ Thread 2 — Calibration

Nobody enjoys this thread and every result depends on it.

▌ Flats, darks, bias: what we settled on

HCAM-2 is a cooled CCD with a well-behaved dark current at −30 °C, so darks are
cheap. Flats are the hard part because FW-8 has eight positions and the survey
uses only V and I, but the other projects use the rest and nobody wants to give
up twilight.

Options considered:

• Twilight flats every night — best quality, costs 25–35 minutes of twilight
  that Meteor Watch and outreach also want.
• Dome flats with the new LED panel — repeatable, available in daytime, colour
  mismatch with the night sky in I.
• Sky flats built from the survey frames themselves — free, but needs enough
  frames per night and masks for stars.

▎ Dome flats in V, twilight flats in I twice a week, and a sky-flat check in
▎ halley every night that shouts if the two disagree by more than half a
▎ percent. That is the whole policy. Please do not make it cleverer.
▎
▎ — Tomasz, calibration meeting, 2026-01-08

Decision: Tomasz's policy, as quoted. The halley check is described in
pipeline-cookbook under "flat drift".

▌ Comparison stars

Each target gets three comparison stars and one check star from the same frame.
Kenji built the sequences; Priya stores them in stacks so halley never reads
them from a spreadsheet again.

                                                                           toml
 # comp-sequences/vss-0117.toml — one target, its comparisons and check star
 [target]
 id = "VSS-0117"
 ra = "06:42:18.3"
 dec = "+22:15:07"
 class = "semi-regular"

 [[comparison]]
 label = "A"
 ra = "06:42:31.9"
 dec = "+22:13:51"
 v = 11.204
 i = 10.388

 [[comparison]]
 label = "B"
 ra = "06:41:58.0"
 dec = "+22:17:40"
 v = 11.871
 i = 11.020

 [check]
 label = "K"
 ra = "06:42:05.2"
 dec = "+22:12:26"
 v = 12.017
 expected_scatter_mmag = 12   # if the check star wanders more than this, the n
↪ight is suspect, not the target


▌ Why three comparisons and not an ensemble of twenty

An ensemble is statistically nicer. It is also much harder to debug at 03:00
when one comparison turns out to be a low-amplitude variable itself, which
happened twice in the first month. Three named stars and one check star is small
enough that Rosa can eyeball the light curves of all four during the night.

▌ Zero points and colour terms

Colour terms are measured on photometric nights from a standard field and stored
per filter per season. We re-measure after anything touches the optical train: a
mirror wash, the FW-8 rebuild, the HCAM-2 window cleaning.

Current values (winter 2026, after the mirror wash): V zero point 22.511, colour
term −0.029; I zero point 21.990, colour term +0.015. The wash bought us 0.09
mag in V, which is about what Tomasz promised.

▌ Open questions for thread 2

• Does the LED dome-flat panel drift with temperature? Tomasz has a logger on
  it; results due in April.
• Do we need a separate flat set for the two weeks a year the dome slit is
  misaligned after the winter service?

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

▌ Thread 3 — Scheduling with nightjar

nightjar is our scheduler: it reads the target list, the cadence rules, the
weather from Sable and the seeing from Quill, and emits a queue that ridge-ctl
works through. Before the survey it only scheduled outreach and ad-hoc
programmes, so this thread is really "teach nightjar about cadence".

▌ The problem

With 400 targets and the cadence table from thread 1, a clear night has more
requests than it has time. On a typical winter night Aster gets about 10.5 hours
of dark sky; the survey wants roughly 14 hours of it. Something has to lose.

▌ Three scheduling strategies

We looked at three:

1. Greedy by urgency — each target has a "due date" from its cadence; pick the
   most overdue observable one next.
2. Block scheduling — split the night into blocks by class (Miras early, RR
   Lyrae runs in the middle, and so on).
3. Nightly optimiser — solve the whole night at dusk with a proper solver and
   replan when the weather changes.

Greedy is a week of work and slews a lot; blocks are two weeks and brittle when
clouds eat a block; the optimiser is six weeks or more and nobody could explain
its choices at 02:00.

▎ I have to be able to look at the queue and say why that star is next. If I
▎ can't, I'll override it, and then it isn't a schedule any more, it's a
▎ suggestion.
▎
▎ — Rosa, scheduling meeting, 2026-01-22

Decision: greedy by urgency, with one exception: RR Lyrae and eclipse runs are
"reservations" that nightjar books first and greedy fills around. That gives us
the protected long runs from option 2 without the brittleness.

▌ Cadence rules as nightjar sees them

                                                                           yaml
 # nightjar/rules/vss.yaml
 programme: vss
 priority_base: 50
 filters: [V, I]
 rules:
   - class: mira
     every_nights: 6
     grace_nights: 3
     exposure: { V: 60, I: 30 }
   - class: semi-regular
     every_nights: 3
     grace_nights: 2
     exposure: { V: 90, I: 45 }
   - class: cepheid
     every_nights: 1
     grace_nights: 1
     exposure: { V: 90, I: 60 }
   - class: rr-lyrae
     reservation: { length_hours: 3, cadence_minutes: 20, max_per_night: 1 }
     exposure: { V: 120, I: 90 }
   - class: eclipsing
     reservation: { around_ephemeris_minima: true, window_hours: 2.5 }
     exposure: { V: 90, I: 60 }
 limits:
   min_altitude_deg: 30
   max_moon_illumination_for_faint: 0.6   # stars fainter than V=14 skip bright
↪-Moon nights
   max_seeing_arcsec: 3.2                 # Quill median over the last ten minu
↪tes


▌ Pilot results (February)

We ran nightjar with these rules for 19 nights in February. Twelve were usable.

┌─────────────────────┬────────┬──────────────┬────────────────────────────────┐
│ Metric              │ Target │ Pilot result │ Comment                        │
╞═════════════════════╪════════╪══════════════╪════════════════════════════════╡
│ Targets observed on │   90 % │         71 % │ Miras fine; semi-regulars      │
│ cadence             │        │              │ behind after the cloudy week   │
├─────────────────────┼────────┼──────────────┼────────────────────────────────┤
│ RR Lyrae runs       │     12 │            9 │ three cut short by cloud, one  │
│ completed           │        │              │ by a dome fault                │
├─────────────────────┼────────┼──────────────┼────────────────────────────────┤
│ Mean slew per       │   < 40 │           52 │ greedy wanders across the      │
│ exposure (s)        │        │              │ meridian; see below            │
├─────────────────────┼────────┼──────────────┼────────────────────────────────┤
│ Nights Rosa         │      0 │            3 │ twice for a bright Moon edge   │
│ overrode the queue  │        │              │ case, once for a satellite     │
│                     │        │              │ pass                           │
├─────────────────────┼────────┼──────────────┼────────────────────────────────┤
│ Queue replans per   │      — │            7 │ mostly weather; fine           │
│ night (median)      │        │              │                                │
└─────────────────────┴────────┴──────────────┴────────────────────────────────┘

▌ Slew overhead

Greedy picks the most overdue star regardless of where it is, so the telescope
zig-zags. A cheap fix: when two candidates are within 10 % of each other in
urgency, prefer the nearer one. Min-jun prototyped it:

                                                                         python
 def pick_next(candidates, pointing, now):
     """Choose the next target: most overdue first, but break near-ties by slew
↪ distance."""
     ranked = sorted(candidates, key=lambda c: c.urgency(now), reverse=True)
     best = ranked[0]
     close = [c for c in ranked if c.urgency(now) >= 0.9 * best.urgency(now)]
     return min(close, key=lambda c: slew_seconds(pointing, c.position(now)))


The replay itself is one query against stacks and one halley command:

                                                                            sql
 SELECT night, count(*) AS frames, avg(slew_s) AS mean_slew FROM frame WHERE ni
↪ght >= '2026-02-01' GROUP BY night;


Early numbers from replaying the February logs: mean slew down from 52 s to 37
s, on-cadence fraction unchanged. Not yet merged; Rosa wants a week of live
nights with it first.

▌ Open questions for thread 3

Short ones first:

• Friday outreach.
• Bright Moon.
• Satellites.

In full:

• How does nightjar share Aster with outreach evenings? Lena needs the first two
  hours on Fridays from April to September.
• Should bright-Moon nights switch the whole survey to I only, rather than
  skipping faint targets?
• The satellite-pass override: is it worth teaching nightjar about it, or is
  Rosa's judgement cheaper?

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

▌ Thread 4 — The halley pipeline

halley already reduced single frames for other projects. For the survey it needs
to do differential photometry at scale, keep every measurement traceable back to
its raw frame, and write to stacks without anyone copying CSV files around.

▌ What landed so far

• Calibration stage reads the policy from thread 2 and records which flat, dark
  and bias it used for every frame.
• Aperture photometry with three apertures per star (2.0, 2.5, 3.0 × FWHM); the
  middle one is the default.
• Differential magnitudes against the three named comparisons, with the check
  star's residual stored alongside.
• A nightly report e-mailed to the survey list: frames in, frames rejected and
  why, stars measured, flat-drift status.
• Writes to stacks in one transaction per night, so a crash leaves nothing
  half-written.

▌ The nightly run

The night-stop script on ridge-ctl kicks halley on ridge-arch once the dome is
closed:

                                                                             sh
 #!/bin/sh
 # night-stop.sh — close up, then hand the night to halley (runs as the obs use
↪r on ridge-ctl)
 set -eu
 NIGHT=$(date -u -d '12 hours ago' +%Y-%m-%d)
 RAW=/data/raw/$NIGHT

 nightjar queue --close --reason "night-stop"
 rsync -a --partial "$RAW/" "ridge-arch:/archive/raw/$NIGHT/"
 ssh ridge-arch "halley run --programme vss --night $NIGHT --config /etc/halley
↪/vss.toml --report survey@example.org"
 echo "night-stop: handed $NIGHT to halley at $(date -u +%H:%M)Z" | logger -t n
↪ight-stop


▌ Schema in stacks

┌─────────────┬─────────────────┬──────────────┬───────────────────────────────┐
│ Table       │ Rows (Mar 2026) │ Key          │ What it holds                 │
╞═════════════╪═════════════════╪══════════════╪═══════════════════════════════╡
│ frame       │          38,204 │ frame_id     │ one row per raw exposure,     │
│             │                 │              │ with filter, airmass, Quill   │
│             │                 │              │ seeing, Sable humidity        │
├─────────────┼─────────────────┼──────────────┼───────────────────────────────┤
│ calibration │          38,204 │ frame_id     │ which master flat, dark and   │
│             │                 │              │ bias were applied             │
├─────────────┼─────────────────┼──────────────┼───────────────────────────────┤
│ measurement │         611,520 │ (frame_id,   │ instrumental magnitudes and   │
│             │                 │ star_id,     │ errors                        │
│             │                 │ aperture)    │                               │
├─────────────┼─────────────────┼──────────────┼───────────────────────────────┤
│ diff_mag    │         203,840 │ (frame_id,   │ differential magnitude,       │
│             │                 │ star_id)     │ check-star residual, quality  │
│             │                 │              │ flag                          │
└─────────────┴─────────────────┴──────────────┴───────────────────────────────┘

▌ Traceability rule

Every row in diff_mag must be reproducible from the raw frame and the config
hash stored with it. Priya's test: pick ten random rows each month, rerun halley
on their frames from scratch, compare to the stored values to 1 mmag. Two months
passed; the third found an airmass rounding bug that was, happily, ours and not
the physics.

▌ Light-curve storage

Measurements plus a cache keyed by star and config hash: fast, and a stale cache
is obvious because the hash changes. Details are in pipeline-cookbook under
"light-curve cache".

▌ Known issues

A typical bad line from the nightly report, for reference:

                                                                           text
 2026-03-04T02:17:44Z halley.reject frame=38112 star=VSS-0231 reason=psf_elonga
↪ted fwhm=3.9 ell=0.31 after_filter_change=62s


• Frames taken during the first 90 s after a filter change sometimes have a
  slightly elongated PSF; halley flags them but does not yet drop them. Suspect
  FW-8 settling. Tomasz is looking at the wheel's detent spring.
• The nightly report says "0 rejected" when the rejection step crashes, which is
  the wrong kind of optimism.
• Stars near the chip edge get the wrong aperture on nights when the guider
  drifts more than 8 px; roughly 0.4 % of rows.

▌ Open questions for thread 4

• Do we reprocess the whole survey when the colour terms change, or only apply a
  correction on read?
• Who is on call for halley when Priya is away? Right now the honest answer is
  "nobody, and it waits".

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

▌ Thread 5 — Publishing

The trust's charter says the data is public. That is easy to agree with and a
lot of work to do properly.

▌ What we publish, and when

┌─────────────┬──────────────┬─────────────┬─────────┬─────────┬───────────────┐
│ Product     │ Audience     │ Format      │ Cadence │ Embargo │ Where         │
╞═════════════╪══════════════╪═════════════╪═════════╪═════════╪═══════════════╡
│ Nightly     │ public,      │ PNG on the  │ every   │ none    │ ridge-web,    │
│ summary     │ volunteers   │ public site │ morning │         │ the "last     │
│ plot        │              │             │         │         │ night" page   │
├─────────────┼──────────────┼─────────────┼─────────┼─────────┼───────────────┤
│ Light       │ researchers  │ CSV + JSON  │ monthly │ 3       │ https://data. │
│ curves (V,  │              │             │         │ months  │ example.org/  │
│ I)          │              │             │         │         │ hro/vss/      │
├─────────────┼──────────────┼─────────────┼─────────┼─────────┼───────────────┤
│ Full        │ researchers  │ Parquet     │ yearly  │ 6       │ https://data. │
│ measurement │              │             │ data    │ months  │ example.org/  │
│ table       │              │             │ release │         │ hro/vss/dr/   │
├─────────────┼──────────────┼─────────────┼─────────┼─────────┼───────────────┤
│ Period      │ researchers, │ CSV + short │ at DR2  │ none    │ the trust's   │
│ catalogue   │ public       │ paper       │ and DR4 │         │ report series │
└─────────────┴──────────────┴─────────────┴─────────┴─────────┴───────────────┘

The longest disagreement in the whole planning process, preserved because we
will have it again next year when someone new joins the team and asks why the
embargo exists at all:

┌──────────────┬───────────┬────────────────────────────────────────┬──────────┐
│ Position     │ Argued by │ Argument                               │ Outcome  │
╞══════════════╪═══════════╪════════════════════════════════════════╪══════════╡
│ No embargo   │ Lena      │ the public paid for the telescope,     │ rejected │
│ at all       │           │ volunteers take half the data, and an  │          │
│              │           │ embargo looks like we have something   │          │
│              │           │ to hide from the good people of the    │          │
│              │           │ town of Marrowby, who can see the dome │          │
│              │           │ from the high street                   │          │
├──────────────┼───────────┼────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────┤
│ Three-month  │ Priya     │ time to catch calibration mistakes     │ adopted  │
│ embargo      │           │ first; a wrong public light curve is   │          │
│              │           │ worse than a late one                  │          │
├──────────────┼───────────┼────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────┤
│ Six-month    │ Kenji     │ the period catalogue needs a full      │ rejected │
│ embargo      │           │ season before anyone else fits it      │          │
└──────────────┴───────────┴────────────────────────────────────────┴──────────┘

▎ I lost the argument and I still think the nightly plot being public from day
▎ one is the most useful thing we do.
▎
▎ — Lena, publishing meeting, 2026-02-12

▌ README that ships with every release

                                                                       markdown
 # HRO Variable Star Survey — data release

 Photometry of 400 variable stars from Halden Ridge Observatory, Aster 0.61 m +
↪ HCAM-2, filters V and I.

 ## Files

 - \`lightcurves/VSS-NNNN.csv\` — one file per star: \`bjd, filter, mag, err, flag
↪\`
 - \`measurements.parquet\` — every differential measurement, with frame and conf
↪ig hashes

 ## Flags

 | Flag | Meaning                               |
 | ---- | ------------------------------------- |
 | 0    | good                                  |
 | 1    | check-star residual above threshold   |
 | 2    | near chip edge, aperture uncertain    |

 Questions: survey@example.org. Please cite the release DOI, not this README.


▌ Open questions for thread 5

• A DOI for each release: which of the free services, and who signs up for the
  account on behalf of the trust?
• Do we publish the bench stars too? They are data, but they are data we decided
  not to trust.
• Yusuf asked whether volunteers who take data should be named as co-authors on
  the catalogue. Ada says yes, in some form; we need a rule before DR1, not
  after.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

▌ Risks

• HCAM-2 cooler fails — spare cooler head on the shelf; swap is a day.
• Priya away for a month — on-call rota for halley, and document the nightly
  run.
• Dunmore refurbishment eats Tomasz — survey work gets Tuesdays, written down.
• Streetlights brighten the sky — Dark Sky Campaign; monthly sky brightness from
  Tern.
• Volunteer fatigue — shorter shifts, a better queue, fewer 03:00 overrides.

⚠️ The last row is the one that ends surveys. Every other risk is hardware.

▌ Timeline

• 2025-11 — kickoff, target cuts.
• 2025-12 — comparison sequences for the first 200 stars.
• 2026-01 — calibration policy, nightjar strategy chosen.
• 2026-02 — nightjar pilot, halley writes to stacks.
• 2026-03 — full 400-star sequences, slew tie-break under test.
• 2026-04 — survey start, officially; everything before is "commissioning".
• 2026-10 — DR0 internal release, embargo clock starts.
• 2027-01 — DR1 public.

