exports[`snapshots of the layout IR as text software/pipeline-cookbook.md @ 80 1`] = `
"── ≡ properties ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 title    halley and nightjar cookbook
 tags     software, halley, nightjar, runbook
 updated  2026-03-14
 owner    Priya Raman
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

▌ halley and nightjar cookbook

Short recipes for the things we actually do at night. Each one is a snippet plus
a line or two of why. The hosts are ridge-ctl (control PC), ridge-arch (archive)
and ridge-web (public site).

If a recipe here disagrees with the code, the code wins and this note is wrong;
please fix the note in the same change rather than leaving a comment in the
channel, because nobody reads the channel at 03:00 and the next night assistant
will trust whatever is written here, in exactly the order it is written, with
exactly the typos it contains, which is how we once parked Aster pointing at the
floor for most of a clear night while the weather station reported perfect
conditions.

▌ When to use this note

• Starting or stopping a night on Aster or Brill.
• Re-running a reduction after a bad flat or a wrong filter in the log.
• Pulling frames or photometry out of the stacks archive.
• Changing the nightjar queue without restarting everything.
• Checking why the public site shows yesterday's all-sky image.

▌ The night pipeline at a glance

One clear night, from the plan to the public site:

                                                                        mermaid
 flowchart LR
     plan[nightjar plan] --> ctl[ridge-ctl: expose]
     ctl --> raw[(raw frames)]
     raw --> halley[halley reduce]
     halley --> phot[photometry]
     phot --> stacks[(stacks on ridge-arch)]
     stacks --> web[ridge-web light curves]


halley reports every star as an instrumental magnitude, so the numbers in
vss_photometry are $m_V$ relative to the comparison stars, not fluxes. The
conversion is the usual one:

$$ m = -2.5 \\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{F}{F_0}\\right) $$

where $F_0$ is the zero-point flux for the filter, fitted each night from the
comparisons.

To see the night's zero points without opening Python, ask stacks directly; the
query is short enough to keep in your head:


 psql stacks -c "select filter, zp, zp_err from zero_points where night = '2026
↪-03-13'"


▌ Start and stop a night

Always start from ridge-ctl, logged in as observer. The start script checks
Sable and Quill before it opens anything, and refuses politely if either is
stale.

                                                                           bash
 ssh observer@ridge-ctl
 night-start --scope aster --plan plans/2026-03-14.toml


Stopping is the same idea; --park is the default but say it anyway so the log is
explicit when someone reads it next week.

                                                                           bash
 night-stop --scope aster --park --close-dome


If the dome refuses to close, do not rerun the script in a loop hoping it
changes its mind. Check the roof interlock first:

                                                                           bash
 ridgectl dome status --verbose | grep -E 'interlock|shutter|rain'


A night with both telescopes looks like this. The && matters: Brill only starts
if Aster did.

                                                                           bash
 night-start --scope aster --plan plans/2026-03-14.toml && night-start --scope
↪brill --plan plans/brill-vss.toml


Emergency stop, for when the rain sensor has already tripped, the scripts are
sulking, and you need the shutter shut now:

                                                                           bash
 ridgectl estop --all --reason "rain, Sable tripped at $(date -u +%H:%M)"


▌ Reduce frames

halley is a normal Python package. For a single night the CLI is enough:

                                                                           bash
 halley reduce --night 2026-03-13 --scope aster --camera hcam2


For anything unusual, drive it from Python. This is the recipe for re-running
one filter with a replacement master flat from the dome screen:

                                                                         python
 from halley import Night, calib

 night = Night.load("2026-03-13", scope="aster")
 flat = calib.master_flat(night.frames(kind="flat", filt="V"), reject="sigma",
↪sigma=3.0)
 for frame in night.frames(kind="science", filt="V"):
     frame.bias().dark().flat(flat).write(overwrite=True)


Photometry for the Variable Star Survey uses fixed apertures. Keep the radii in
pixels, not arcseconds, or the archive disagrees:

                                                                         python
 from halley.phot import aperture

 result = aperture(frame, targets=vss.targets_for(frame), r_in=6.0, r_out=12.0,
↪ r_sky=(16.0, 24.0))
 result.to_stacks(table="vss_photometry", night=frame.night, if_exists="replace
↪_night_and_filter_only")


▌ Query the stacks archive

stacks is PostgreSQL on ridge-arch. Read-only access is enough for almost
everything:

                                                                           bash
 psql "postgresql://reader@ridge-arch.example.invalid/stacks"


Frames from one night, newest first:

                                                                            sql
 SELECT frame_id, filt, exptime, airmass, fwhm_arcsec
 FROM frames
 WHERE night = '2026-03-13' AND scope = 'aster'
 ORDER BY obs_start DESC;


The light curve for one VSS star, with the bad-seeing nights removed:

                                                                            sql
 SELECT p.mjd, p.mag, p.mag_err
 FROM vss_photometry p
 JOIN frames f USING (frame_id)
 WHERE p.star = 'VSS-0217' AND f.fwhm_arcsec < 3.5 AND p.flags = 0 AND f.filt =
↪ 'V' AND p.mjd > 60700
 ORDER BY p.mjd;


▌ Configure halley and nightjar

halley reads halley.toml from the working directory. Paths are relative to the
data root unless they start with a slash.

The deployed copy is halley.toml, checked against its schema; the season's
targets are halley/targets.yaml, and the reduction itself is halley/reduce.py,
run by night.sh.

                                                                           toml
 [paths]
 data_root = "/data/halley"
 calib = "calib"

 [aster]
 camera = "hcam2"
 gain_e_per_adu = 1.42
 saturation_adu = 58000


nightjar keeps its queue rules in YAML. Priorities are integers; higher runs
first, and ties go to the target at lower airmass.

                                                                           yaml
 queue:
   - target: VSS-0217
     scope: aster
     priority: 80
     filters: [B, V, R]
     cadence: 2h
   - target: M42-outreach
     scope: corvid
     priority: 20


Weather limits live next to the queue. Quill's seeing is in arcseconds:

                                                                           yaml
 limits:
   wind_max_kmh: 40
   humidity_max_pct: 85
   seeing_max_arcsec: 4.0


Per-host overrides go in a small TOML file next to the main one. It is fenced
with tildes here because the comment inside quotes a triple backtick from the
chat where it was agreed:

                                                                           toml
 # halley.local.toml — ridge-arch only. Agreed in chat as \`\`\`reduce_workers = 6
↪\`\`\`, then halved.
 [runtime]
 reduce_workers = 3
 scratch = "/scratch/halley"


▌ Talk to nightjar over HTTP

nightjar has a tiny HTTP API on ridge-ctl:8420. Its full description is the
OpenAPI spec; how it runs is nightjar/deploy.yaml (per environment:
nightjar/environments.yaml), and its manifest is package.json. Adding a target
to tonight's queue:

                                                                           json
 {
   "target": "VSS-0342",
   "scope": "brill",
   "priority": 60,
   "filters": ["V", "I"],
   "exptime_s": 120,
   "requested_by": "Kenji Mori"
 }


                                                                           bash
 curl -X POST -H 'Content-Type: application/json' --data @vss-0342.json http://
↪ridge-ctl.example.invalid:8420/queue


▌ Build and deploy

The archive tools ship as a container. The Makefile target builds and tags it:

                                                                       makefile
 IMAGE := stacks-tools
 TAG   := $(shell git describe --tags --always)

 image:
     docker build -t $(IMAGE):$(TAG) -f docker/Dockerfile .


The calibration library has its own tiny Makefile on ridge-arch. Recipe lines
must start with a real tab, which is why this block is copied from the file
rather than retyped:

                                                                           make
 calib: masters/bias.fits masters/dark.fits

 masters/%.fits: raw/%/*.fits
     halley calib build --kind $* --out $@ $^
     @echo "built $@ from $(words $^) frames"


                                                                     dockerfile
 FROM python:3.12-slim
 WORKDIR /app
 COPY . .
 RUN pip install --no-cache-dir .[archive]
 ENTRYPOINT ["stacks-tools"]


▌ The public site

ridge-web shows the latest Tern all-sky frame. The page polls a JSON file rather
than the archive:

                                                                     typescript
 async function latestSky(): Promise<string> {
   const res = await fetch("https://ridge.example.org/sky/latest.json", { cache
↪: "no-store" });
   const body: { url: string; taken: string } = await res.json();
   return body.url;
 }


If the image is stale, the exporter on ridge-arch has usually stopped. Its log
line looks like:


 2026-03-14T02:11:09Z tern-export: skipped frame 88213 (exposure incomplete)


▌ A fix worth remembering

The bias level drifted after the HCAM-2 firmware update. This is the change we
made to halley.toml after two nights of odd-looking residuals:

                                                                           diff
  [aster]
  camera = "hcam2"
 -bias_level_adu = 1010
 +bias_level_adu = 1024
  gain_e_per_adu = 1.42


▌ Things that are not code but look like it

The night log format, for reference; nightjar writes one line per exposure:


 02:14:33  aster  VSS-0217  V  60s  airmass 1.21  fwhm 2.8"
 02:16:01  aster  VSS-0217  R  45s  airmass 1.21  fwhm 2.7"


The directory layout under the data root:


 /data/halley/
   raw/2026-03-13/aster/
   calib/2026-03-13/
   reduced/2026-03-13/aster/


▌ Safety rules

• Always write the reason into the night log before overriding nightjar.
• Keep the queue file in git; a hand edit on ridge-ctl is lost at the next
  deploy.
• Never run night-start from a laptop over the visitor Wi-Fi.
• Never delete rows from frames; mark them with flags instead.
• Do not change nightjar priorities while an exposure longer than five minutes
  is running.
• Tell Rosa (or whoever is the night assistant) before you touch the dome from
  home.
• Leave Dunmore out of every queue until the refurbishment is signed off.
• Ask Priya before changing any column in vss_photometry.
• Min-jun's test scripts run against a copy of stacks, never the live one.

Tomasz asked that this be written down plainly: the dome motor controller does
not care what the software thinks the shutter position is, and if the encoder
slipped during the day the only reliable check is to look at the camera in the
dome or walk up the ridge with a torch, which is annoying at 02:00 in February
but far cheaper than a new shutter seal, a new motor, and an awkward
conversation with the trust about why the insurance excess is our problem this
year.

▌ Check that it all works

One command at the end of setup, from ridge-ctl:

                                                                           bash
 halley doctor && nightjar check --queue queue.yaml && ridgectl dome status
"
`;

